WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:11.960
Hello, everyone, and welcome
to lecture 2 of CME 295.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.200
So today is an exciting
day because we'll

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.120
be talking about
large language models,

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:20.480
and in particular, how they
relate to the transformer

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.640
architecture that
we saw last lecture.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.100
But before we start, we will
recap what we saw last time.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.040
And we will do that
at every lecture

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.800
to see how this connect
to what we'll see today.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.160
So if you remember, last
time we were wondering

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.280
how models could process text.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.600
So models, they
don't understand text

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.160
as it is, they
understand numbers.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.680
So if you remember, one
thing that we looked at

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:55.800
was, first of all, how to divide
our text into indivisible units.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.800
And this process is
called tokenization.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.780
And we saw a few kinds of
tokenization algorithms.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:06.300
And we said that the most
common tokenization algorithm

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.500
these days is the subword
tokenization because it

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.780
has a nice trade-off
between the vocabulary

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:18.100
that it was building
and the sequence length

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:21.540
that input text would have.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.980
But this was not
it, because once you

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:27.180
divide a text into
tokens, the next step

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:29.500
is to compute embeddings.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:33.300
So if you remember, we
saw Word2Vec as a way

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:37.700
to compute embeddings, but they
were not aware of the context.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:41.900
So then we talked about another
class of models that were very

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.580
popular in the 2010s
which is called RNNs,

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:48.380
Recurrent Neural Networks.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:51.580
And then what we said
was, OK, the problem

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:55.140
is that you tend
to forget things

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.680
that happened in the past.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.480
And so in 2017, there was this
landmark paper called "Attention

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:07.360
is All You Need" that
actually dropped the principle

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:13.920
of recurrence and had direct
connections in order to compute

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:17.160
meaningful
representation of tokens.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.140
So we spent a little bit
of time on some notations.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:26.100
So if you remember, we
had query, key, and value,

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.820
which were useful
for us to know OK,

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:35.080
given a token, what other tokens
should that token attend to?

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:39.480
And we saw that the architecture
that the authors introduced

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:44.640
was called the transformer, and
that the attention operation

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.000
that is here is
actually something

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.240
that is happening in
the multi-head attention

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:56.580
layers, which are present in
the encoder and the decoder.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:01.380
And what we said was that this
architecture was first developed

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.000
in the context of
machine translation,

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:11.460
in that in that context, that
model performed really well.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.780
So a natural question
is, well, given

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:16.980
that this architecture
performs really, really

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:21.580
well for machine translation,
can we actually propagate

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:25.460
the gains to other domains?

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:29.720
And so this is what we're
going to see in the first part.

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:32.980
So we're going to talk
about different variants

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:36.300
of the original transformer
architecture, such

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:41.980
that it was possible to
apply it to other domains.

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:44.860
So if you recall,
the transformer

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:47.700
is composed of two main parts.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.580
The first part is
the encoder, which is

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.100
on the left side of the figure.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:59.760
And the goal of the encoder is
to compute meaningful embeddings

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.160
of your input text.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.620
So here it was in the context
of machine translation.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:07.920
So input text, you can think
of it as the source text

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.200
that you want to translate.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.880
And then you had a second
part on the right side, which

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:20.959
is the decoder whose goal is to
actually generate the output one

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:25.120
token at a time in an
autoregressive manner.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:28.840
And the way it does
that is by attending

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:33.080
to all the tokens that
it has predicted so far,

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:37.640
plus the meaningful
embeddings from the input that

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:41.160
were computed by the encoder.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:43.600
So that was the
original transformer.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.980
So a very natural
question is, well,

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:50.740
how about we try to play
with the architecture?

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:53.130
We try to see,
well, what happens

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:56.170
if we only take the encoder?

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:58.810
Let's suppose we only
take the encoder.

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:01.690
Can we do something with it?

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:05.690
Well, this is something that
people have tried shortly

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:10.970
after the transformer came out
in 2018 with a very important

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:16.210
paper called BERT, which stands
for Bidirectional Encoder

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:19.330
Representation
from Transformers.

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:28.290
So the idea is to take only the
encoder parts of the transformer

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:34.370
and to leverage the multi-head
attention layer which

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:39.810
performs the self-attention
operation over all of its inputs

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:47.810
in order to have all the input
tokens attend to one another.

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:50.190
And so here what
they say is, well,

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:54.730
if you do that and if you
introduce a special token--

00:05:54.730 --> 00:05:58.070
and here the special token is
on the left side of the sequence

00:05:58.070 --> 00:06:00.430
called CLS token.

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:02.750
So it's a classification token.

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:09.030
Then what you could do is
rely on the meaningful encoded

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:13.470
embedding corresponding
to the CLS token

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:18.390
in order to know what the
input sequence is about.

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:22.190
Because here the encoder
is doing self-attention

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:24.150
across all the tokens.

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:27.230
So the meaningful
embedding that is

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:31.670
corresponding to the
CLS token has interacted

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:35.190
with all other tokens.

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:42.990
So the idea of BERT is to say if
you have some input text, then

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:47.470
what I can do is to compute
a meaningful representation

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:51.850
of the input and then
use that representation

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:54.290
for some downstream task.

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:57.850
So at the time we had something
like sentiment extraction

00:06:57.850 --> 00:06:59.630
as a very common task.

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:01.790
So is this tweet
positive or not?

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:03.850
So typically what
you could do is

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:07.010
take that embedding and then
project that onto that class.

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:08.370
That's one example.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:12.590
But then you could also take
the embedding of a given token,

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:15.370
and then try to determine
if that token is

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:19.070
corresponding to let's say,
a noun or a verb and so on.

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:24.570
So that's the idea.

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:27.690
Does that make sense?

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:29.130
OK, great.

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:32.010
So now the next question
is, how do you actually

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:34.130
train such a model?

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:37.490
Well, this is done in two steps.

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:41.050
The first step is
called pre-training.

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:48.090
And the goal is for the model to
understand how text is written.

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:52.310
And so the way you do that
is with some proxy tasks.

00:07:52.310 --> 00:07:56.710
So if you remember, a proxy task
is a task that is not really

00:07:56.710 --> 00:07:59.470
your end goal, but
that is a way for you

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:01.690
to achieve something else.

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:03.670
And for us, this
something else here

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:08.110
is the model understanding text.

00:08:08.110 --> 00:08:12.670
So the first proxy task that
this model is trained with

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:18.430
is called Masked
Language Modeling, MLM.

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:24.710
And the idea is for
the model to understand

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:27.070
how tokens are placed.

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:31.790
And so the way it does that
is it takes the input text

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:34.750
and then it corrupts
it randomly.

00:08:34.750 --> 00:08:39.429
So the way it does that is it
selects some tokens at random

00:08:39.429 --> 00:08:41.372
and it masks them.

00:08:41.372 --> 00:08:42.830
So actually, it's
a little bit more

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:45.170
complicated to not
introduce biases.

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:51.170
But the idea is to corrupt
the input sequence.

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:57.250
And the idea is to compute
meaningful embeddings

00:08:57.250 --> 00:09:02.130
corresponding to these
corrupted tokens in order

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:08.610
to try to predict the actual
token-- the original token

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:13.090
that was before that
corrupted token.

00:09:13.090 --> 00:09:14.930
So here, as an
example, if we take

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:17.730
a cute Teddy Bear is reading and
let's suppose we corrupt cute

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:21.290
and we corrupt
reading, then here

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:24.930
our goal could be to compute
a meaningful representation

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:28.450
of these corrupted
tokens and try

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:32.930
to predict the original token.

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:35.170
And what the model
will try to do

00:09:35.170 --> 00:09:39.690
is to rely on all the
attention mechanism

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:44.170
that it is performing
in order to deduce

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:50.470
what the original token
was from the context.

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:53.230
So in other words,
the model is trying

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:55.750
to understand how
language is written

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:57.590
and trying to
understand, what are

00:09:57.590 --> 00:10:02.550
the words that are likely to be
in places where it is missing?

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:04.370
So that's the first proxy task.

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:08.310
And then there's a second proxy
task that the authors of BERT

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:12.950
have also tried, which is
called NSP, which stands

00:10:12.950 --> 00:10:16.790
for Next Sentence Prediction.

00:10:16.790 --> 00:10:19.230
And the idea behind
that proxy task

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:28.070
is for the model to understand
how sentences are sequenced.

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:33.350
So here the idea is to have two
different sentences as input,

00:10:33.350 --> 00:10:37.150
and to ask the model if
these two sentences actually

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:40.030
follow each other or not.

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:43.330
So in practice, what you would
do is you would look at a text

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:47.310
and take two sentences that
follow each other, put it there,

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:49.890
try to predict that it
is indeed following.

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:52.630
And then two sentences that
are not following each other

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:55.770
and you need to predict
that it's not following.

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:59.370
And the idea behind
that proxy task

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:04.870
is for the model to learn text,
not at the sentence level,

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:08.010
but at the document level.

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:11.290
So that's the first step
called pre-training.

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:15.610
And at that stage, you're
assuming that your model now

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:19.090
understands text much better.

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:22.640
And that's why you can
go to the second task.

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:25.290
So by the way, the first task
is typically very expensive,

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:29.930
something you do just once,
train on a bunch of data.

00:11:29.930 --> 00:11:33.370
And then the second
task is for you

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:36.570
to adapt your model to
the specific use case

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:38.810
that you're interested in.

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:42.590
So if we take the example of
sentiment extraction, which

00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:46.690
is trying to predict
if a sentence is,

00:11:46.690 --> 00:11:50.390
let's say, positive or
negative, what you would do

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:58.230
is have some projection layer
that takes in the CLS token that

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:03.390
is carrying all the
information from the sentence

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:05.750
and project it onto the class--

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:10.510
the classes of interests which
are positive or negative.

00:12:10.510 --> 00:12:16.090
And for that, you need to
have a small set of labels.

00:12:16.090 --> 00:12:19.630
So you need the observations
with actual labels.

00:12:19.630 --> 00:12:22.470
And then what you would
do is to tune the weights,

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:26.510
so that you can actually project
that meaningful representation

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:28.370
onto the space of interest.

00:12:31.030 --> 00:12:33.970
So what are the pros and
cons of this approach?

00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:38.790
So the pro is, well, it led
to pretty good performance.

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:43.350
The other thing is it is relying
on the self-attention mechanism.

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:47.530
So you have connections from
both sides, which is actually

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:49.750
the reason why BERT
is called that way,

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:53.010
because it's Bidirectional
Encoder Representation

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:55.850
from Transformers.

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:58.830
Another pro is, well, you
need to do fine tuning,

00:12:58.830 --> 00:13:04.090
but you don't need that much
data typically or of 100.

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:08.210
But the con is, well, you
need a small amount of data

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:10.950
to do the fine-tuning, but you
need to do the fine-tuning.

00:13:10.950 --> 00:13:13.610
So in itself, that's an effort.

00:13:13.610 --> 00:13:15.690
So that's a con.

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:20.610
And then the other con is
when you look at this model,

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:26.450
you may be wondering, well,
how can I generate text?

00:13:26.450 --> 00:13:29.690
And it's something
that is not obvious.

00:13:29.690 --> 00:13:33.410
But actually, we
will see in lecture 8

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:36.810
that it is actually
possible to generate text

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:39.390
with an encoder only model.

00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:43.430
But for now, let's suppose
there's no obvious way.

00:13:43.430 --> 00:13:45.130
But more on that in lecture 8.

00:13:47.990 --> 00:13:50.350
Cool.

00:13:50.350 --> 00:13:53.150
So that's for
encoder only model.

00:13:53.150 --> 00:13:56.630
Well, going back to the
transformer architecture,

00:13:56.630 --> 00:14:00.150
we tried removing the decoder,
only going with the encoder.

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:01.750
That's what we obtained.

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:04.470
Now how about we
remove the encoder

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:07.470
and we try with
just the decoder?

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:09.470
Well, let's just try that.

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:13.190
Well, actually, people
have tried that roughly

00:14:13.190 --> 00:14:16.230
at the same time as
BERT was published.

00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:19.910
There was another branch
of transformer variants

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:24.750
which were actually focusing
on decoder only transformers.

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:27.230
And this is the
example of GPT, which

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:29.950
I'm sure you have
heard plenty of times,

00:14:29.950 --> 00:14:34.430
which stands for Generative
Pre-trained Transformer.

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:41.160
And the idea is to only keep the
decoder parts of the transformer

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:50.520
and formulates any task you
have as a text-to-text problem.

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:52.080
So what does that mean?

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:55.080
That means you have
some text as input

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:58.560
and your only job is to
predict the next token.

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:06.320
So here beginning of sequence
A, A, teddy, teddy bear,

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:10.000
is, reading, and so on.

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:12.800
So by the way, this
kind of decoding

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:15.600
is called autoregressive
decoding because you

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:18.880
predict tokens one at a time.

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:20.740
And this is the idea behind GPT.

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:24.440
So you don't need to do--
you don't necessarily

00:15:24.440 --> 00:15:28.400
need to do any fine-tuning
because your model is already

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:33.960
equipped to be adapted
to any use case.

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:35.300
So I'll give you an example.

00:15:35.300 --> 00:15:36.800
The sentiment extraction.

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:40.300
So let's assume you have a
tweet you want to categorize

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:43.380
as positive or negative.

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:46.900
Well, you would just
put into your model,

00:15:46.900 --> 00:15:49.890
is this sentence
positive or negative?

00:15:49.890 --> 00:15:51.140
And then you put the sentence.

00:15:51.140 --> 00:15:56.660
And then the model would just
respond positive or negative.

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:58.420
You don't need to
do fine-tuning,

00:15:58.420 --> 00:16:01.980
unless you want to make
your model even better.

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:06.620
So fine-tuning is not
a required step, which

00:16:06.620 --> 00:16:08.080
is already pretty appealing.

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:16.180
But you may be wondering,
well, last time we

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:19.200
saw the encoder-decoder
architecture,

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:22.580
the original transformer,
which was also generating text.

00:16:22.580 --> 00:16:25.700
Well, what is the difference
between this new decoder

00:16:25.700 --> 00:16:29.940
only model with the
encoder-decoder model?

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:36.120
Well, so both are similar in
that they can generate text

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:38.240
autoregressively.

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:42.600
But in the case of the
encoder-decoder architecture,

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:47.720
the input has a
special treatment.

00:16:47.720 --> 00:16:52.000
It is input into the encoder,
and within that encoder,

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:57.040
all the tokens are able to
interact with one another.

00:16:57.040 --> 00:17:00.080
So it's like pure
self-attention.

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:03.680
Whereas in the decoder
only architecture,

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:06.480
what you have as
input, the tokens

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:11.319
can only interact between
themselves and tokens

00:17:11.319 --> 00:17:12.760
before them.

00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:16.839
They cannot interact
with tokens after them.

00:17:16.839 --> 00:17:20.619
So you have causal
attention for decoder only,

00:17:20.619 --> 00:17:24.880
but you have bidirectional
attention for encoder-decoder

00:17:24.880 --> 00:17:27.520
for the input.

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:29.240
That's the first difference.

00:17:29.240 --> 00:17:35.460
The second difference is that,
well, you have an extra encoder

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:40.840
and you need to have some
special care for the input.

00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:43.100
You're treating it differently,
so it may make sense

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:45.180
for things like
machine translation

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:49.620
because, well, you have
source texts as inputs

00:17:49.620 --> 00:17:53.340
and you want to have
target text as outputs.

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:57.380
But things like text
generation, it's not super clear

00:17:57.380 --> 00:17:58.640
what should be as input.

00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:05.780
So in other words, decoder only
is a little bit more general

00:18:05.780 --> 00:18:10.980
because it treats
everything the same.

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:14.860
So people have tried
both approaches

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:18.460
when it came to the
text-to-text paradigm,

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:21.940
and overwhelmingly
they all pretty much

00:18:21.940 --> 00:18:24.340
went with the decoder only.

00:18:24.340 --> 00:18:26.940
So what I want
you to remember is

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:30.300
that people have tried
all different variants.

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:34.440
And in particular when it
comes to generating texts,

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:36.340
there was both these paradigms.

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:41.160
But the decoder only
one is the one that

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:44.380
actually lived through today.

00:18:44.380 --> 00:18:46.800
And one of the reasons
is, well, it's simpler

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:49.320
and there is no special
treatment of the inputs.

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:52.800
And yeah, you don't
need to have an encoder

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:55.880
to treat the input separately.

00:18:55.880 --> 00:18:58.220
So overall, there
are two paradigms.

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:00.440
Either you compute
meaningful embeddings

00:19:00.440 --> 00:19:03.120
and you try to fine-tuning
for your use case,

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:06.520
or you frame it as a
text to text problem.

00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:10.240
And you can frame all problems
as text-to-text problems.

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:11.823
Another question
is, is it efficient?

00:19:11.823 --> 00:19:12.740
It's another question.

00:19:12.740 --> 00:19:14.800
But you can do that.

00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:18.300
And that's what people
have been doing so far.

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:23.800
Cool.

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:28.280
Well, as I mentioned,
people went

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:30.980
with the decoder only
architecture, and what they did

00:19:30.980 --> 00:19:33.260
was just to scale
that up, because they

00:19:33.260 --> 00:19:36.380
have seen that it was
performing better and better.

00:19:36.380 --> 00:19:40.540
And in particular,
in the early 2020s,

00:19:40.540 --> 00:19:44.860
there were a couple of papers
that actually quantified how

00:19:44.860 --> 00:19:49.180
much better your model was
getting as you were training it

00:19:49.180 --> 00:19:55.660
on more data, with more
parameters, with more compute.

00:19:55.660 --> 00:19:57.940
And we'll see that now.

00:19:57.940 --> 00:20:02.420
So this paper from
2020 is actually

00:20:02.420 --> 00:20:09.300
saying that you can actually
have almost like a law that

00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:12.460
tells you how much
better your model will

00:20:12.460 --> 00:20:16.580
get as a function of
the number of tokens

00:20:16.580 --> 00:20:18.100
that you're training on.

00:20:18.100 --> 00:20:21.460
So it's the middle
chart, and as a function

00:20:21.460 --> 00:20:28.120
of how big your model is,
which is the rightmost chart.

00:20:28.120 --> 00:20:32.800
And so that's the reason
why in 2020, 2021, 2022,

00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:36.000
people have been
increasing model size

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:40.800
and training it on more
tokens like increasingly.

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:45.280
And not only that,
the authors also

00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:50.560
saw that the bigger your
model, the more token efficient

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:52.640
they were as well.

00:20:52.640 --> 00:20:54.120
So what does that mean?

00:20:54.120 --> 00:20:58.040
That means that if you
have a bigger model,

00:20:58.040 --> 00:21:01.600
you need a fewer
amount of tokens

00:21:01.600 --> 00:21:06.880
to train on in order to
reach a certain performance.

00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:09.020
So on the slide, so what
are all these colors?

00:21:09.020 --> 00:21:11.500
So on the y-axis,
it's the Tesla's.

00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:13.440
So it's the performance
of your model.

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:15.240
The lower the better.

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:17.680
And then the x-axis
is the number

00:21:17.680 --> 00:21:22.560
of tokens that were processed
as part of the training.

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:24.640
And so the beautiful
colors, they're

00:21:24.640 --> 00:21:30.100
saying that the
brighter you are,

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:35.140
the bigger your model is, the
more it tends to have a better

00:21:35.140 --> 00:21:38.740
performance earlier.

00:21:38.740 --> 00:21:41.940
So that's why we're saying
that bigger models are also

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:43.000
more token efficient.

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:48.380
They need fewer training
tokens to perform better.

00:21:48.380 --> 00:21:53.320
But as you can imagine,
well, hardware is limited.

00:21:53.320 --> 00:21:55.700
You don't have infinite compute.

00:21:55.700 --> 00:21:58.520
So very naturally,
you're wondering, well,

00:21:58.520 --> 00:22:01.740
if I have a certain
compute budget,

00:22:01.740 --> 00:22:05.340
how much should I
scale my model in size?

00:22:05.340 --> 00:22:08.900
And how much should I attribute
that to the number of tokens

00:22:08.900 --> 00:22:11.300
I should train it on?

00:22:11.300 --> 00:22:14.980
So before we talk about this, I
want to introduce some notations

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:16.680
that I think maybe
you have seen,

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:19.780
but I want to make sure
we're all on the same page.

00:22:19.780 --> 00:22:24.600
So the first one is FLOPs,
which stands for Floating Point

00:22:24.600 --> 00:22:25.540
Operations.

00:22:25.540 --> 00:22:28.800
And please note the little
S, lowercase S. So this

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:33.200
is a unit that is meant
to measure compute.

00:22:33.200 --> 00:22:38.320
So the higher the FLOPs, the
more compute you actually have.

00:22:38.320 --> 00:22:41.160
So order of magnitude, so
training FLOPs for LLMs

00:22:41.160 --> 00:22:44.720
is like 10 to the power of 25.

00:22:44.720 --> 00:22:47.160
But you may see another
FLOPS out there.

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:51.880
So FLOPS with a
capital S or even /S.

00:22:51.880 --> 00:22:55.760
And that one is actually
the compute speed.

00:22:55.760 --> 00:23:00.000
And it's actually a measure of
how performant your hardware is.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:03.960
It is different compared
to the previous FLOPs,

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:05.540
but it's easy to get mixed up.

00:23:05.540 --> 00:23:08.680
So just wanted to call that out.

00:23:08.680 --> 00:23:12.780
So with that said, back
to what we were saying.

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:16.580
So we live in a world where we
don't have unlimited compute,

00:23:16.580 --> 00:23:18.200
or at least not yet.

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:22.280
So given the amount of compute
that you have available,

00:23:22.280 --> 00:23:25.560
a natural question
that you have is,

00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:28.880
how much should I
increase my model size?

00:23:28.880 --> 00:23:33.540
Versus how much should I
increase the training size

00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:36.540
on which I'm training my model?

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:38.620
And so there is this
paper that came out

00:23:38.620 --> 00:23:44.460
in 2022 which exactly
asked this question.

00:23:44.460 --> 00:23:47.460
And what it found was
actually pretty interesting.

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:53.740
It found that there was actually
a sweet spot between model size

00:23:53.740 --> 00:23:57.420
and training data size.

00:23:57.420 --> 00:24:01.900
And it also found that most
models at the time in 2022

00:24:01.900 --> 00:24:05.180
were actually
undertrained, meaning

00:24:05.180 --> 00:24:10.460
that they were too big
for the amount of tokens

00:24:10.460 --> 00:24:11.820
that they were trained on.

00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:14.300
They were undertrained.

00:24:14.300 --> 00:24:18.940
And so it actually also showed
that by training two models.

00:24:18.940 --> 00:24:22.670
So one, which was, I believe,
70 billion parameters

00:24:22.670 --> 00:24:26.170
and another one 200 and
something billion parameters,

00:24:26.170 --> 00:24:28.950
so higher than the previous one.

00:24:28.950 --> 00:24:32.990
And it had the same
compute budget for the two.

00:24:32.990 --> 00:24:36.090
So the first one, given
it's smaller in model size,

00:24:36.090 --> 00:24:39.590
it trained it on more tokens.

00:24:39.590 --> 00:24:43.335
I believe it trained it
on $1.4 trillion tokens.

00:24:43.335 --> 00:24:45.210
And the second one,
which was bigger in size,

00:24:45.210 --> 00:24:47.350
it trained it on fewer
tokens because it

00:24:47.350 --> 00:24:49.990
was the same compute budget.

00:24:49.990 --> 00:24:54.310
And it showed that the
smaller model in terms of size

00:24:54.310 --> 00:24:57.230
was actually more performant.

00:24:57.230 --> 00:25:01.870
So they had some relationship
between number of parameters

00:25:01.870 --> 00:25:04.890
and number of tokens that
you are training it on,

00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:10.790
which was something like
one parameter for 20 tokens.

00:25:10.790 --> 00:25:12.670
And so this was quite important.

00:25:12.670 --> 00:25:15.590
A lot of people tried
to go back and see OK,

00:25:15.590 --> 00:25:18.870
is my model compute optimal?

00:25:18.870 --> 00:25:23.810
So yeah, so this is also
pretty important paper.

00:25:23.810 --> 00:25:25.830
Another question you
can ask yourself--

00:25:25.830 --> 00:25:28.770
I mean, just trying to
think beyond this paper

00:25:28.770 --> 00:25:30.890
is what your use case is.

00:25:30.890 --> 00:25:35.550
Because here we're talking
about training compute optimal.

00:25:35.550 --> 00:25:41.090
But you also need to serve
your model production.

00:25:41.090 --> 00:25:45.310
So if you serve it with
many, many requests,

00:25:45.310 --> 00:25:50.690
you also have an incentive
to not have it be too big.

00:25:50.690 --> 00:25:52.190
But that's another question.

00:25:52.190 --> 00:25:54.730
But I just want to put it out.

00:25:54.730 --> 00:25:56.650
Yeah.

00:25:56.650 --> 00:25:58.910
So the question is, is it
the cross-entropy loss?

00:25:58.910 --> 00:26:00.810
And the answer is
yes, because your task

00:26:00.810 --> 00:26:02.750
is next token prediction.

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:06.490
And what you're
trying to do is have

00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:09.890
your prediction be as
close as the label, which

00:26:09.890 --> 00:26:10.877
is the next token.

00:26:10.877 --> 00:26:13.210
And yes, cross-entropy is
typically the one that you use

00:26:13.210 --> 00:26:16.610
and it's the one that is here.

00:26:16.610 --> 00:26:19.430
Yeah.

00:26:19.430 --> 00:26:22.470
So the question is, does the
quality of the data matter?

00:26:22.470 --> 00:26:24.070
I would say there
are many papers that

00:26:24.070 --> 00:26:29.570
came out from this, many
with different conclusions.

00:26:29.570 --> 00:26:31.870
I would say for
pre-training, what matters is

00:26:31.870 --> 00:26:34.390
the size more than the quality.

00:26:34.390 --> 00:26:39.190
We will see in lecture 3 that
next steps of the training

00:26:39.190 --> 00:26:43.150
process matters, there's more
importance towards quality

00:26:43.150 --> 00:26:46.590
and we will see that.

00:26:46.590 --> 00:26:49.430
OK, cool.

00:26:49.430 --> 00:26:51.170
OK, moving on to LLM.

00:26:51.170 --> 00:26:54.750
So why do we call LLM, LLM?

00:26:54.750 --> 00:26:59.750
Well LLM stands for
Large Language Model.

00:26:59.750 --> 00:27:01.550
So language model
is, because it's

00:27:01.550 --> 00:27:06.350
a model that associates some
probabilities to sequences

00:27:06.350 --> 00:27:07.590
of tokens.

00:27:07.590 --> 00:27:10.350
So in this case, it's
a model that tries

00:27:10.350 --> 00:27:12.270
to predict the next token.

00:27:12.270 --> 00:27:16.070
So we're trying to put some
probability towards what

00:27:16.070 --> 00:27:19.290
the next prediction is.

00:27:19.290 --> 00:27:20.430
And why is it large?

00:27:20.430 --> 00:27:23.930
Well, it is large because the
model size is large, typically

00:27:23.930 --> 00:27:27.730
in the billions, even tens of
billions or hundreds of billions

00:27:27.730 --> 00:27:30.650
and even trillions.

00:27:30.650 --> 00:27:33.110
Training data, also hundreds
of billions of tokens.

00:27:33.110 --> 00:27:37.650
At least nowadays, it's also on
the tens of trillions of tokens.

00:27:37.650 --> 00:27:38.790
And also compute.

00:27:38.790 --> 00:27:41.690
You need a lot of GPUs.

00:27:41.690 --> 00:27:46.730
And as I mentioned,
nowadays people

00:27:46.730 --> 00:27:49.950
rely on the decoder
only transformer,

00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:55.610
which is the original
transformer without the encoder.

00:27:55.610 --> 00:28:01.250
Well, you may ask me, well,
if you look into the decoder,

00:28:01.250 --> 00:28:04.130
you actually have
a component, which

00:28:04.130 --> 00:28:07.810
is the cross-attention
that takes

00:28:07.810 --> 00:28:13.210
in the meaningful embeddings
from the encoder with the ones

00:28:13.210 --> 00:28:14.230
coming from the decoder.

00:28:14.230 --> 00:28:16.730
And you're like, OK, what is
happening with that components?

00:28:16.730 --> 00:28:18.670
Well, you also remove it.

00:28:18.670 --> 00:28:20.610
So the decoder is not
exactly the decoder.

00:28:20.610 --> 00:28:24.590
You also remove the
part that is interacting

00:28:24.590 --> 00:28:29.230
and you obtain this
very simple backbone,

00:28:29.230 --> 00:28:34.830
which is a causal multi-head
attention layer, which is only

00:28:34.830 --> 00:28:37.790
attending to each token
and the tokens that

00:28:37.790 --> 00:28:42.750
came before it and a feed
forward neural network, which

00:28:42.750 --> 00:28:44.290
is where the compute happens.

00:28:47.110 --> 00:28:49.070
Cool.

00:28:49.070 --> 00:28:50.070
OK.

00:28:50.070 --> 00:28:55.030
So what we saw here is the
bigger your model size,

00:28:55.030 --> 00:28:57.230
the better your performance.

00:28:57.230 --> 00:29:00.390
And we saw that there was also
a trade-off between the number

00:29:00.390 --> 00:29:02.070
of tokens you were
training it on

00:29:02.070 --> 00:29:05.150
and the model size
that you were having.

00:29:05.150 --> 00:29:11.270
A natural question could
be, when you have an input

00:29:11.270 --> 00:29:16.370
x into your model, if
your model is huge,

00:29:16.370 --> 00:29:19.490
then that means that there are
a lot of computations happening

00:29:19.490 --> 00:29:21.490
in that model.

00:29:21.490 --> 00:29:24.690
A natural question
can be do you really

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:31.930
need all the parameters in your
model for that given input x?

00:29:31.930 --> 00:29:34.050
Do you really need that?

00:29:34.050 --> 00:29:37.890
And I want to motivate
this observation

00:29:37.890 --> 00:29:40.810
with another observation.

00:29:40.810 --> 00:29:44.330
So let's assume I'm here
in a room full of people.

00:29:44.330 --> 00:29:47.450
And let's say I have
a math question.

00:29:47.450 --> 00:29:50.850
Let's suppose you all are coming
from different backgrounds.

00:29:50.850 --> 00:29:52.510
So let's assume
you're a math expert.

00:29:52.510 --> 00:29:55.450
Let's assume you're a physics
expert, biology expert.

00:29:55.450 --> 00:29:59.690
And I'm coming with
my math question.

00:29:59.690 --> 00:30:00.830
Should I ask everyone?

00:30:00.830 --> 00:30:03.050
Should I only ask you?

00:30:03.050 --> 00:30:05.410
And this is exactly
the kind of question

00:30:05.410 --> 00:30:08.650
that people have
asked themselves.

00:30:08.650 --> 00:30:14.190
How about we actually
only activate

00:30:14.190 --> 00:30:18.230
a subset of the model for
given inputs as opposed

00:30:18.230 --> 00:30:22.230
to all parameters?

00:30:22.230 --> 00:30:24.990
And so if you were
to do that, then you

00:30:24.990 --> 00:30:28.790
could scale your
model in size, but not

00:30:28.790 --> 00:30:34.350
incur the cost of having to go
through all these operations

00:30:34.350 --> 00:30:37.790
for every forward pass?

00:30:37.790 --> 00:30:39.830
So how about you did that?

00:30:39.830 --> 00:30:43.310
Well, it turns out there's
a type of model out there

00:30:43.310 --> 00:30:46.230
that was actually
predating LLMs, which

00:30:46.230 --> 00:30:49.830
is called a mixture of experts.

00:30:49.830 --> 00:30:52.450
So who has heard of
mixture of experts?

00:30:52.450 --> 00:30:52.950
Yes.

00:30:52.950 --> 00:30:54.150
OK, great.

00:30:54.150 --> 00:30:56.450
So what is the idea
behind mixture of experts?

00:30:56.450 --> 00:30:58.470
So you have experts.

00:30:58.470 --> 00:31:00.330
So think of it as
your math expert,

00:31:00.330 --> 00:31:02.430
physics experts, et cetera.

00:31:02.430 --> 00:31:08.430
And then you have a gate,
also called a router,

00:31:08.430 --> 00:31:14.050
that tries to figure out, given
your input, which expert should

00:31:14.050 --> 00:31:18.770
you use and to what extent?

00:31:18.770 --> 00:31:21.530
So for instance, you
have some input x

00:31:21.530 --> 00:31:24.150
and you're passing it through
your gate or your router.

00:31:24.150 --> 00:31:26.730
And the router
says OK, expert 2,

00:31:26.730 --> 00:31:28.750
so this is a math
question I should ask you.

00:31:31.330 --> 00:31:38.210
So there are several kinds of
Mixture of Experts or MoEs.

00:31:38.210 --> 00:31:41.690
One first kind is
called a dense MoE.

00:31:41.690 --> 00:31:43.730
A dense MoE, what does it do?

00:31:43.730 --> 00:31:46.850
It still activates
all the experts,

00:31:46.850 --> 00:31:49.690
but it gives more
weight towards experts

00:31:49.690 --> 00:31:51.930
that are maybe more relevant.

00:31:51.930 --> 00:31:53.490
So, for instance,
the math question

00:31:53.490 --> 00:31:56.690
could be high weight for
the math expert, maybe lower

00:31:56.690 --> 00:31:59.890
weight for the physics expert,
but maybe a very small weight

00:31:59.890 --> 00:32:03.730
for the biology experts.

00:32:03.730 --> 00:32:07.090
So the bad thing
with the dense MoE

00:32:07.090 --> 00:32:09.210
is you still activate
all the parameters.

00:32:09.210 --> 00:32:10.510
It's not what you want to do.

00:32:10.510 --> 00:32:15.030
So that's why, in
the context of LLMs,

00:32:15.030 --> 00:32:17.830
the kind that is
actually very interesting

00:32:17.830 --> 00:32:19.590
is actually not
the dense MoE, it's

00:32:19.590 --> 00:32:25.670
the sparse MoE, which is the
kind of ammo that actually

00:32:25.670 --> 00:32:30.870
selects experts that
should be activated

00:32:30.870 --> 00:32:35.670
and that ignores all
the other experts.

00:32:35.670 --> 00:32:37.130
This is the sparse MoE.

00:32:37.130 --> 00:32:41.590
So typically you have top k
experts that were selected.

00:32:41.590 --> 00:32:45.870
So k can be equal to 1, can
be equal to 2 to some number

00:32:45.870 --> 00:32:48.270
that you fix.

00:32:48.270 --> 00:32:54.510
And the output that you get, so
y hat is the sum of the expert

00:32:54.510 --> 00:32:58.110
output weighted by how
important they are,

00:32:58.110 --> 00:33:01.510
which is given by the gates.

00:33:01.510 --> 00:33:06.290
And it's only a sum over some
experts that were activated--

00:33:06.290 --> 00:33:08.540
so k experts, not all experts.

00:33:11.140 --> 00:33:11.640
Cool.

00:33:11.640 --> 00:33:12.380
Well, OK.

00:33:12.380 --> 00:33:13.440
All that is great.

00:33:13.440 --> 00:33:15.500
But now let's go
back to reality.

00:33:15.500 --> 00:33:16.880
Let's go back to our LLM.

00:33:16.880 --> 00:33:20.300
If you remember, it's a
decoder only architecture.

00:33:20.300 --> 00:33:23.580
So we have this as a backbone.

00:33:23.580 --> 00:33:26.000
So I have a question for you.

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:31.880
Where would you put this
mixture of experts scheme

00:33:31.880 --> 00:33:33.620
in the architecture?

00:33:33.620 --> 00:33:37.160
Which layer would you put it?

00:33:37.160 --> 00:33:39.860
Does anyone have
any suggestions?

00:33:44.920 --> 00:33:47.880
Yes.

00:33:47.880 --> 00:33:48.380
Yep.

00:33:48.380 --> 00:33:51.680
So the answer here is
you can take a token

00:33:51.680 --> 00:33:54.260
and project it in order to
select which expert it is.

00:33:54.260 --> 00:33:54.760
Yes.

00:33:54.760 --> 00:33:57.760
So that is actually
the role of the router.

00:33:57.760 --> 00:33:59.840
So here the thing that
I'm really interested in

00:33:59.840 --> 00:34:03.600
is where would you put the
whole mixture of experts

00:34:03.600 --> 00:34:04.580
in all of this?

00:34:04.580 --> 00:34:05.540
So I'll help you.

00:34:05.540 --> 00:34:06.040
Oh, yeah.

00:34:06.040 --> 00:34:06.540
Sorry.

00:34:06.540 --> 00:34:08.699
Yeah.

00:34:08.699 --> 00:34:09.880
It's a great answer.

00:34:09.880 --> 00:34:12.760
So I'll just exactly
mention what you said.

00:34:12.760 --> 00:34:15.639
So the answer would be
feedforward neural network,

00:34:15.639 --> 00:34:19.699
because this is where all the
compute typically resides.

00:34:19.699 --> 00:34:22.020
And yes, I completely
agree with you.

00:34:22.020 --> 00:34:26.179
The multi-head attention
layer is actually the place

00:34:26.179 --> 00:34:27.880
where tokens communicate.

00:34:27.880 --> 00:34:31.980
It's not where typically you
compute meaningful embeddings

00:34:31.980 --> 00:34:33.940
by projecting it.

00:34:33.940 --> 00:34:37.480
So you would typically go with
the feedforward neural network.

00:34:37.480 --> 00:34:38.580
So a great answer.

00:34:38.580 --> 00:34:41.679
And so here what you would
do is back to your point,

00:34:41.679 --> 00:34:44.820
have a router where
for a given input,

00:34:44.820 --> 00:34:48.880
decides which
expert to activate.

00:34:48.880 --> 00:34:54.739
And here the expert would be
a feedforward neural network.

00:34:54.739 --> 00:34:58.120
So yeah, great answer.

00:34:58.120 --> 00:34:58.620
Sorry.

00:34:58.620 --> 00:34:59.120
Yeah.

00:35:01.860 --> 00:35:05.640
So the question is, well,
or the remark I would say

00:35:05.640 --> 00:35:09.360
is that in the attention layer,
you have different heads.

00:35:09.360 --> 00:35:13.280
Why wouldn't you treat
heads as experts?

00:35:13.280 --> 00:35:14.740
So it's a great point.

00:35:14.740 --> 00:35:19.580
I think, at the end of the
day, you can also try that.

00:35:19.580 --> 00:35:21.640
But speaking of that,
we will actually

00:35:21.640 --> 00:35:26.160
talk about a small variation
that people usually

00:35:26.160 --> 00:35:29.800
do on multi-head attention
because, not all heads are

00:35:29.800 --> 00:35:30.920
as useful.

00:35:30.920 --> 00:35:32.800
So I'm going to just
address your question

00:35:32.800 --> 00:35:35.600
in about 30 minutes.

00:35:35.600 --> 00:35:37.300
Yeah, but great point.

00:35:37.300 --> 00:35:37.800
Yep.

00:35:40.640 --> 00:35:44.360
So the question is, how
does the router know

00:35:44.360 --> 00:35:45.940
when to activate each expert?

00:35:45.940 --> 00:35:50.160
So by with the magic of back
propagation, magic of learning.

00:35:50.160 --> 00:35:53.400
So you have a training
process and then

00:35:53.400 --> 00:35:58.280
you want to learn how each
component should behave.

00:35:58.280 --> 00:36:01.920
And you just backpropagate
and it just learns that way.

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:07.160
And it's actually a great
point because one observation

00:36:07.160 --> 00:36:10.500
you may tell me is,
what would prevent

00:36:10.500 --> 00:36:16.540
your model from overrelying
on a specific set of experts

00:36:16.540 --> 00:36:20.540
and not explore other experts?

00:36:20.540 --> 00:36:23.460
And so this is
actually a problem

00:36:23.460 --> 00:36:27.460
when training
MoE-based LLMs, which

00:36:27.460 --> 00:36:31.260
is the tendency for
your model to rely only

00:36:31.260 --> 00:36:36.180
on a small set of experts
instead of all the experts.

00:36:36.180 --> 00:36:38.860
And it's called
routing collapse.

00:36:38.860 --> 00:36:40.980
So there are many reasons
why it's not good.

00:36:40.980 --> 00:36:45.140
But one of the reasons is
your effective model size

00:36:45.140 --> 00:36:51.100
is actually smaller than
the total number of experts

00:36:51.100 --> 00:36:52.740
and weights that you have.

00:36:52.740 --> 00:36:57.900
And this typically
occurs, because when

00:36:57.900 --> 00:37:02.520
you train your model, if
some experts get chosen

00:37:02.520 --> 00:37:05.640
in the very beginning,
then when it

00:37:05.640 --> 00:37:11.240
comes to updating the
weights, those experts that

00:37:11.240 --> 00:37:13.680
were activated in
the beginning, they

00:37:13.680 --> 00:37:18.000
tend to be updated at first.

00:37:18.000 --> 00:37:20.520
So they tend to get
better and better,

00:37:20.520 --> 00:37:24.560
whereas all the other experts
are still sitting there.

00:37:24.560 --> 00:37:28.640
So it's the problem of
the rich getting richer.

00:37:28.640 --> 00:37:33.280
And so it's for that reason
that people have also come up

00:37:33.280 --> 00:37:37.800
with a part of the
last cult, so it's

00:37:37.800 --> 00:37:42.080
a load balancing
auxiliary loss, which

00:37:42.080 --> 00:37:49.320
is aimed at forcing your model
to also incentivize exploring

00:37:49.320 --> 00:37:56.320
other experts by encouraging
the fraction of the tokens that

00:37:56.320 --> 00:38:00.620
are being rerouted to be
uniform or more or less uniform

00:38:00.620 --> 00:38:02.660
across experts.

00:38:02.660 --> 00:38:04.640
And so maybe it's something
that I didn't say,

00:38:04.640 --> 00:38:06.820
but I will say it now.

00:38:06.820 --> 00:38:10.340
The routing is done
at the token level.

00:38:10.340 --> 00:38:12.820
So if you have an
input sentence,

00:38:12.820 --> 00:38:18.700
each token will ask itself,
which expert should I go to?

00:38:18.700 --> 00:38:21.220
So this is done at
the token level.

00:38:21.220 --> 00:38:26.440
And so here this loss is
incentivizing your model-- yeah.

00:38:29.060 --> 00:38:31.400
So the question is, well,
if you select one or two,

00:38:31.400 --> 00:38:32.400
it's not differentiable.

00:38:32.400 --> 00:38:33.280
It's a great point.

00:38:33.280 --> 00:38:37.220
So you have an operator
that's called stop grad that

00:38:37.220 --> 00:38:42.620
is treating some aspects as
being actually derivative of 0

00:38:42.620 --> 00:38:45.220
and treating these as constants.

00:38:45.220 --> 00:38:48.340
So long story short
is in the loss,

00:38:48.340 --> 00:38:52.100
you're treating the fraction
of tokens routed to expert i

00:38:52.100 --> 00:38:54.260
as a constant.

00:38:54.260 --> 00:38:58.580
And you're only backpropagating
through the routing probability.

00:38:58.580 --> 00:39:02.480
So you're still able to
actually change those routers.

00:39:02.480 --> 00:39:04.540
But yeah, that's a good point.

00:39:08.200 --> 00:39:09.200
So let me see.

00:39:09.200 --> 00:39:12.200
So I think we're
talking about the fact

00:39:12.200 --> 00:39:17.400
that these auxiliary loss
is incentivizing your model

00:39:17.400 --> 00:39:21.400
to also choose other
experts and not

00:39:21.400 --> 00:39:24.120
to have this routing
collapse problem that we

00:39:24.120 --> 00:39:27.237
mentioned at the beginning.

00:39:27.237 --> 00:39:28.820
There are some other
problems as well.

00:39:28.820 --> 00:39:31.880
I just want to just tell
you that training an MoE

00:39:31.880 --> 00:39:38.400
based LLM is challenging, and
this is part of its challenge.

00:39:38.400 --> 00:39:44.000
But the good thing is, if you
do it properly, you will have--

00:39:44.000 --> 00:39:46.960
so here on the
slide, what you see

00:39:46.960 --> 00:39:52.640
is an input text where
the color of each section

00:39:52.640 --> 00:39:58.020
denotes a specific experts
that a given token was sent to.

00:39:58.020 --> 00:40:00.780
And this is, for instance,
at a given layer.

00:40:00.780 --> 00:40:03.880
And this is coming from
a paper from Mixtral.

00:40:03.880 --> 00:40:07.260
So here is the model
called Mixtral of experts.

00:40:07.260 --> 00:40:10.760
And you see that the
colors are pretty diverse.

00:40:10.760 --> 00:40:14.940
So it means that you're actually
leveraging your experts pretty

00:40:14.940 --> 00:40:17.220
well.

00:40:17.220 --> 00:40:22.280
And so I want to just say a few
things about MoE-based LLMs.

00:40:22.280 --> 00:40:23.760
So now we're in 2026.

00:40:23.760 --> 00:40:25.900
This is something that
I think the paper was

00:40:25.900 --> 00:40:31.140
from 2024, which is quite
some time ago in the AI field.

00:40:31.140 --> 00:40:35.580
So nowadays, what people
do is they use experts,

00:40:35.580 --> 00:40:40.340
but they also have
shared experts

00:40:40.340 --> 00:40:46.420
that gets activated no matter
which experts the router

00:40:46.420 --> 00:40:48.380
or the gate selects.

00:40:48.380 --> 00:40:51.460
And the idea behind
this is for every token,

00:40:51.460 --> 00:40:56.280
there is always some computation
that is always necessary.

00:40:56.280 --> 00:41:00.520
And you want to avoid your
experts from always learning

00:41:00.520 --> 00:41:04.120
the same thing as a foundation
and then specializing

00:41:04.120 --> 00:41:06.400
into something.

00:41:06.400 --> 00:41:08.380
So that is something
that people do nowadays.

00:41:08.380 --> 00:41:12.220
The second thing that people
do is the number of experts,

00:41:12.220 --> 00:41:15.920
they don't choose
it to be 8 or 16.

00:41:15.920 --> 00:41:21.240
They actually choose it to be
higher, 60 or in the hundreds.

00:41:21.240 --> 00:41:27.120
And the reason why they do
that is that intuitively, you

00:41:27.120 --> 00:41:29.120
just give the chance
to your experts

00:41:29.120 --> 00:41:32.440
to be very specialized,
very fine-grained.

00:41:32.440 --> 00:41:35.640
But also on the charts, you
see that the performance,

00:41:35.640 --> 00:41:40.880
when you have more fine-grained
experts is just higher.

00:41:40.880 --> 00:41:43.960
So it's also just empirical.

00:41:43.960 --> 00:41:47.360
And so speaking of
order of magnitudes.

00:41:47.360 --> 00:41:51.920
So when it comes to the
frontier open source LLMs

00:41:51.920 --> 00:41:54.040
that were published recently.

00:41:54.040 --> 00:41:58.340
So here the screenshot is
actually from a few days ago.

00:41:58.340 --> 00:42:01.140
If we take the top
models, what we see

00:42:01.140 --> 00:42:03.800
is that-- so there
are MoE-based LLMs.

00:42:03.800 --> 00:42:05.940
And the number of
experts that they have

00:42:05.940 --> 00:42:08.760
is on the order of hundreds.

00:42:08.760 --> 00:42:12.020
So here are 384, 256.

00:42:12.020 --> 00:42:14.700
And the number of
experts that are

00:42:14.700 --> 00:42:20.460
activated at each forward
pass is typically eight or so.

00:42:20.460 --> 00:42:22.940
And you also have
a shared expert,

00:42:22.940 --> 00:42:26.580
which is not widely used.

00:42:26.580 --> 00:42:30.260
Like in this example, the
first model is not using it.

00:42:30.260 --> 00:42:32.180
And in terms of
the model size, it

00:42:32.180 --> 00:42:35.500
is in the hundreds of
billions of parameters

00:42:35.500 --> 00:42:39.080
or even trillion parameter
for the first one.

00:42:39.080 --> 00:42:41.860
So it's pretty big models.

00:42:41.860 --> 00:42:43.998
Yes.

00:42:43.998 --> 00:42:45.040
So it's a great question.

00:42:45.040 --> 00:42:48.340
The question is, does
the scaling law still

00:42:48.340 --> 00:42:51.550
hold true for a MoE-based LLMs?

00:42:51.550 --> 00:42:57.490
So the answer is that paper was
actually based on a dense LLM.

00:42:57.490 --> 00:42:59.830
It was not like
an MoE-based LLM.

00:42:59.830 --> 00:43:03.390
So one thing that
I've seen papers do

00:43:03.390 --> 00:43:06.110
is have these scaling
law experiments

00:43:06.110 --> 00:43:11.710
for their own setup on small
number of tokens, small model

00:43:11.710 --> 00:43:15.790
size to just see what the law
is for the specific setup.

00:43:15.790 --> 00:43:19.870
So to your question, I
think for MoE-based LLMs,

00:43:19.870 --> 00:43:25.510
if authors feel that
their choice, their design

00:43:25.510 --> 00:43:29.510
is very different from what was
tested before, they would just

00:43:29.510 --> 00:43:32.070
do this very small
experiment and see what

00:43:32.070 --> 00:43:35.330
is the kind of law
that comes from there,

00:43:35.330 --> 00:43:39.910
and then choose the
parameters from there.

00:43:39.910 --> 00:43:42.030
Yep.

00:43:42.030 --> 00:43:42.570
Yep.

00:43:42.570 --> 00:43:46.510
So the question is, why
is n the number of experts

00:43:46.510 --> 00:43:47.810
doing in the numerator?

00:43:47.810 --> 00:43:51.130
So here, actually,
what you're doing is

00:43:51.130 --> 00:43:53.810
you are summing
across the experts.

00:43:53.810 --> 00:43:56.450
And so if you actually
write that mathematically,

00:43:56.450 --> 00:44:01.530
you actually want to add n to--

00:44:01.530 --> 00:44:05.690
because fi and Pi is like over
n, so it's over n squared,

00:44:05.690 --> 00:44:07.010
times n is like over n.

00:44:07.010 --> 00:44:09.770
So it's times n.

00:44:09.770 --> 00:44:13.650
But n is the number of experts.

00:44:13.650 --> 00:44:17.330
OK, great.

00:44:17.330 --> 00:44:18.510
We're doing amazing.

00:44:18.510 --> 00:44:21.030
Any questions, other questions?

00:44:24.050 --> 00:44:26.570
So far, what did we see?

00:44:26.570 --> 00:44:30.610
We saw that people have
tried different variants

00:44:30.610 --> 00:44:31.710
of the transformer.

00:44:31.710 --> 00:44:34.410
They saw OK, how about
we take the encoder?

00:44:34.410 --> 00:44:35.750
Well, OK, you can do this.

00:44:35.750 --> 00:44:37.470
How about you take the decoder?

00:44:37.470 --> 00:44:40.530
You treat that as a
text-to-text problem.

00:44:40.530 --> 00:44:41.770
They scale that up.

00:44:41.770 --> 00:44:44.410
And people have seen
that the performance just

00:44:44.410 --> 00:44:46.810
gets better and better
as people increase it

00:44:46.810 --> 00:44:50.910
in size and in number of
tokens that it is trained on.

00:44:50.910 --> 00:44:53.550
We've seen that when
the model is too big,

00:44:53.550 --> 00:44:57.150
you need to do something
in order for you to not

00:44:57.150 --> 00:45:00.110
have crazy inference costs.

00:45:00.110 --> 00:45:04.750
And so that's why people have
developed a MoE-based LLMs that

00:45:04.750 --> 00:45:07.470
allow you to scale the
number of parameters

00:45:07.470 --> 00:45:11.430
without having to incur
the cost of also activating

00:45:11.430 --> 00:45:15.030
all the parameters
in each forward pass.

00:45:15.030 --> 00:45:18.710
So here in this part,
what we will focus on

00:45:18.710 --> 00:45:22.030
is a number of
changes that people

00:45:22.030 --> 00:45:28.270
have made in the layers
that were originally

00:45:28.270 --> 00:45:33.990
described in the original
transformer architecture.

00:45:33.990 --> 00:45:36.830
So you will see in a
second what I mean.

00:45:36.830 --> 00:45:41.070
So if you remember, the
transformer architecture

00:45:41.070 --> 00:45:44.950
relies on the
self-attention mechanism,

00:45:44.950 --> 00:45:50.490
which is the idea of letting
all your tokens communicate

00:45:50.490 --> 00:45:53.010
with everyone.

00:45:53.010 --> 00:45:56.170
So the problem
when you do that is

00:45:56.170 --> 00:45:58.930
that you lose the
information of where

00:45:58.930 --> 00:46:03.130
tokens are positioned because
you have these direct links.

00:46:03.130 --> 00:46:06.010
So if you don't have any
information about positions

00:46:06.010 --> 00:46:10.330
about each of these tokens
that are included anywhere,

00:46:10.330 --> 00:46:16.810
well, you lose the information
of where tokens are located.

00:46:16.810 --> 00:46:23.010
So the original transformer
paper tried two approaches.

00:46:23.010 --> 00:46:27.650
The first was learning
position embeddings

00:46:27.650 --> 00:46:31.810
and adding those embeddings
to the token embeddings.

00:46:31.810 --> 00:46:36.450
So what people would do is
you have an input sequence,

00:46:36.450 --> 00:46:38.290
you have your tokens.

00:46:38.290 --> 00:46:42.450
Each token is associated
with a position index.

00:46:42.450 --> 00:46:45.910
Let's assume 1, 2, 3, and so on.

00:46:45.910 --> 00:46:51.510
And here the idea is to learn
an embedding that is associated

00:46:51.510 --> 00:46:53.670
with each position index.

00:46:53.670 --> 00:46:55.610
So position 1 will
have an embedding,

00:46:55.610 --> 00:46:59.790
position 2 will have an
embedding, and so on.

00:46:59.790 --> 00:47:02.950
And these embeddings
would be learned.

00:47:02.950 --> 00:47:06.870
And what would happen is,
in order for your model

00:47:06.870 --> 00:47:11.110
to actually know about
where each of these tokens

00:47:11.110 --> 00:47:15.470
are located, so if you see on
the right, what people would do

00:47:15.470 --> 00:47:19.150
is take the token
embedding and then

00:47:19.150 --> 00:47:23.590
add that position-specific
embedding to the token

00:47:23.590 --> 00:47:29.790
just to reflect that token is
at a very specific position.

00:47:29.790 --> 00:47:32.230
But the problem
with this approach

00:47:32.230 --> 00:47:37.470
is, as we mentioned last time,
that if you train your model

00:47:37.470 --> 00:47:43.730
on a given length of
input data, if you ever

00:47:43.730 --> 00:47:48.470
come across some sequence
at inference time, that is,

00:47:48.470 --> 00:47:52.170
let's say longer, the
problem is actually

00:47:52.170 --> 00:47:55.810
have never encountered
positions beyond the ones

00:47:55.810 --> 00:47:59.170
that you encountered
at training time.

00:47:59.170 --> 00:48:02.770
So you actually do not know what
embeddings for such position

00:48:02.770 --> 00:48:03.530
are.

00:48:03.530 --> 00:48:06.010
You don't know.

00:48:06.010 --> 00:48:09.810
And so that's the reason
why the original authors

00:48:09.810 --> 00:48:13.950
of the transformer paper, they
not only tried the learned way,

00:48:13.950 --> 00:48:18.610
but they also tried a
deterministic formula-based way

00:48:18.610 --> 00:48:21.170
that we're going
to see in a second.

00:48:21.170 --> 00:48:23.530
And the reason why they
tried this approach

00:48:23.530 --> 00:48:31.650
is because it can generalize
to any sequence length

00:48:31.650 --> 00:48:37.490
without having you to actually
train on longer sequences.

00:48:37.490 --> 00:48:39.730
And they actually said
that both approaches

00:48:39.730 --> 00:48:42.950
were roughly leading to
the same performance.

00:48:42.950 --> 00:48:48.750
So the fixed one is better
and we're going to see it now.

00:48:48.750 --> 00:48:53.550
So there was this analogy that I
mentioned last time that I will

00:48:53.550 --> 00:48:56.770
start from in this session.

00:48:56.770 --> 00:49:03.150
So the idea behind the
deterministic formulation

00:49:03.150 --> 00:49:11.550
is to express each position
on a high dimensional vector.

00:49:11.550 --> 00:49:17.270
And what you want is that
vector to exactly inform you

00:49:17.270 --> 00:49:20.750
in which position you're in.

00:49:20.750 --> 00:49:24.450
And so you want that.

00:49:24.450 --> 00:49:30.910
You also want two
positions to be embedded,

00:49:30.910 --> 00:49:35.430
such that the similarity
operation between these two

00:49:35.430 --> 00:49:41.130
embeddings are high if
the positions are close

00:49:41.130 --> 00:49:44.810
and it's low when these
positions are far.

00:49:44.810 --> 00:49:47.570
And so I'm going
to just motivate

00:49:47.570 --> 00:49:50.530
how the authors have
actually designed

00:49:50.530 --> 00:49:52.530
the formula and the
design that they're

00:49:52.530 --> 00:49:58.090
using for the position embedding
that they're proposing here.

00:49:58.090 --> 00:50:01.450
So going through the
analogy that we discussed,

00:50:01.450 --> 00:50:05.250
let's assume you're
looking at your watch.

00:50:05.250 --> 00:50:06.910
You have several hands.

00:50:06.910 --> 00:50:08.468
You have the hour hand.

00:50:08.468 --> 00:50:09.510
You have the minute hand.

00:50:09.510 --> 00:50:12.450
You have the second hand.

00:50:12.450 --> 00:50:14.823
If you put all these
hands together,

00:50:14.823 --> 00:50:19.486
you know exactly
what time it is.

00:50:19.486 --> 00:50:26.170
And each of these hands, they're
moving at different frequencies.

00:50:26.170 --> 00:50:28.790
So the hour tells you roughly
when you are in the day,

00:50:28.790 --> 00:50:30.750
the minute is a little
bit more precise.

00:50:30.750 --> 00:50:33.890
And then the second is
like, even more precise.

00:50:33.890 --> 00:50:37.250
And so what we're
going to do is do

00:50:37.250 --> 00:50:41.990
something very similar for the
position embeddings, where we'll

00:50:41.990 --> 00:50:49.110
have a function of higher
and lower frequency,

00:50:49.110 --> 00:50:54.250
such that when you put all of
that together into one vector,

00:50:54.250 --> 00:50:59.310
if you know exactly
which position you're at.

00:50:59.310 --> 00:51:02.610
So it's going to get a little
bit more into the math.

00:51:02.610 --> 00:51:05.190
So disclaimer, I
hope you remember

00:51:05.190 --> 00:51:06.650
your trigonometry formula.

00:51:06.650 --> 00:51:08.330
But if not, don't worry.

00:51:08.330 --> 00:51:10.430
We'll also look at it together.

00:51:10.430 --> 00:51:16.950
So let's assume that
you are at position m.

00:51:16.950 --> 00:51:22.170
And so here you're looking
at two consecutive indices--

00:51:22.170 --> 00:51:25.750
so 2i and 2i plus 1.

00:51:25.750 --> 00:51:31.970
So what the authors propose
is to have one be a cosine,

00:51:31.970 --> 00:51:35.190
the other one be a
sine or vice versa,

00:51:35.190 --> 00:51:40.160
and have the frequency
of these functions

00:51:40.160 --> 00:51:43.520
be a function of the index.

00:51:43.520 --> 00:51:49.000
So here, if i is low, then
you have a higher frequency

00:51:49.000 --> 00:51:50.760
function.

00:51:50.760 --> 00:51:55.720
If i is high, you have a
lower frequency function,

00:51:55.720 --> 00:52:00.560
meaning your sine
will vary more slowly.

00:52:00.560 --> 00:52:02.000
So here, if you
see on the graph,

00:52:02.000 --> 00:52:06.780
I'm not sure if you
exactly read the axis.

00:52:06.780 --> 00:52:10.340
So on the right
side of the slide,

00:52:10.340 --> 00:52:15.080
you have on the
y-axis the position,

00:52:15.080 --> 00:52:18.960
and on the x-axis,
the dimension.

00:52:18.960 --> 00:52:24.680
So here what you see is
if you take the first row

00:52:24.680 --> 00:52:29.360
and you're moving down,
you're going from position 1

00:52:29.360 --> 00:52:34.000
to later positions
of your sequence.

00:52:34.000 --> 00:52:37.160
And you see that the first
dimensions, the first indices,

00:52:37.160 --> 00:52:41.740
the one on the left, they
tend to vary very quickly,

00:52:41.740 --> 00:52:44.200
and the one on the
right, the later indices,

00:52:44.200 --> 00:52:47.900
they tend to vary
much, much more slowly.

00:52:47.900 --> 00:52:50.940
So in other words,
your lower indices,

00:52:50.940 --> 00:52:53.800
they're much higher frequency.

00:52:53.800 --> 00:52:58.260
And the later indices, they
are of much lower frequency.

00:52:58.260 --> 00:53:02.020
So just to tell you that this
seemingly scary formula is just

00:53:02.020 --> 00:53:03.180
telling you this.

00:53:03.180 --> 00:53:07.780
So it's varying cosine, sine
with different frequencies.

00:53:07.780 --> 00:53:11.180
It's a higher frequency for a
lower index, lower frequency

00:53:11.180 --> 00:53:14.180
for a higher index.

00:53:14.180 --> 00:53:19.060
So let's assume you note
omega i, the huge formula,

00:53:19.060 --> 00:53:21.420
so that we don't need
to worry about it.

00:53:21.420 --> 00:53:27.180
Well, what happens if you have
a position m and a position n?

00:53:27.180 --> 00:53:29.300
How do you compute
the similarity?

00:53:29.300 --> 00:53:32.202
Well, you take the dot product.

00:53:32.202 --> 00:53:33.660
So if you remember
the way you take

00:53:33.660 --> 00:53:39.960
the dot product of two vectors
is you multiply each dimension

00:53:39.960 --> 00:53:43.040
and then you add everything up.

00:53:43.040 --> 00:53:47.660
So just a refresher
on trigonometry.

00:53:47.660 --> 00:53:51.840
So cosine of a minus b
is cosine of a cosine

00:53:51.840 --> 00:53:59.040
of b plus sine of a sine of b
just the trigonometry formula.

00:53:59.040 --> 00:54:06.680
So here if you take the cosine
of omega i of m minus n,

00:54:06.680 --> 00:54:13.700
it's actually the dot product
between these two indices.

00:54:19.560 --> 00:54:22.140
So the question, is i a
function of the dimension?

00:54:22.140 --> 00:54:22.640
Yes.

00:54:22.640 --> 00:54:24.760
So i is a function of the index.

00:54:24.760 --> 00:54:29.840
So if you have a lower
i, then your omega i

00:54:29.840 --> 00:54:33.460
will be such that it will
be of higher frequency.

00:54:33.460 --> 00:54:36.180
So yes, it's a
function of the index.

00:54:36.180 --> 00:54:37.500
That's a great question.

00:54:37.500 --> 00:54:43.140
So here going back to our
favorite trigonometric formula,

00:54:43.140 --> 00:54:49.500
so here I just want to point out
that cosine of omega i m minus n

00:54:49.500 --> 00:54:55.500
is equal to the dot
product of these two.

00:54:55.500 --> 00:54:59.300
And if you actually want to take
the dot product of the whole two

00:54:59.300 --> 00:55:04.300
vectors, you
actually obtain a sum

00:55:04.300 --> 00:55:12.500
of the cosine of the omega i
of all the indices multiplied

00:55:12.500 --> 00:55:16.540
by m minus n.

00:55:16.540 --> 00:55:19.020
Is everyone following so far?

00:55:19.020 --> 00:55:20.660
Yeah.

00:55:20.660 --> 00:55:22.380
So why am I saying this?

00:55:22.380 --> 00:55:26.660
I'm saying this because the
dot product of the two position

00:55:26.660 --> 00:55:32.520
embeddings at position m and
n is a function of m minus n.

00:55:32.520 --> 00:55:38.280
And you may remember
that cosine of 0

00:55:38.280 --> 00:55:41.480
is the maximum value
that cosine can take.

00:55:41.480 --> 00:55:45.760
So in other words,
when m is equal to n,

00:55:45.760 --> 00:55:47.100
the sum is maximized.

00:55:47.100 --> 00:55:50.680
So the similarity is maximum.

00:55:50.680 --> 00:55:56.240
So all of that to say that if
you take this expression that

00:55:56.240 --> 00:56:00.040
is graphically something that
you see on the left in terms

00:56:00.040 --> 00:56:04.000
of the value of the embeddings,
what you will obtain

00:56:04.000 --> 00:56:07.500
is that positions that
are close together,

00:56:07.500 --> 00:56:10.800
they're actually of high
similarity and positions that

00:56:10.800 --> 00:56:14.800
are farther together, they
are of lower similarity.

00:56:14.800 --> 00:56:16.800
And that's what you
see on the right.

00:56:16.800 --> 00:56:21.080
So on the right, you have
the positions on the x-axis

00:56:21.080 --> 00:56:24.640
and on the y-axis of m and n.

00:56:24.640 --> 00:56:28.840
And you see that when
the positions are equal,

00:56:28.840 --> 00:56:32.980
the similarity is maximum.

00:56:32.980 --> 00:56:36.380
So all of that to say that if
you were to look at the formulas

00:56:36.380 --> 00:56:39.660
without any context, I think
you'd be probably scared.

00:56:39.660 --> 00:56:44.460
But I hope that with
this perspective,

00:56:44.460 --> 00:56:48.720
it's a little bit less scary.

00:56:48.720 --> 00:56:55.560
So now what I want to tell you
is that if everything was great,

00:56:55.560 --> 00:56:59.300
we would be using this
exact method today.

00:56:59.300 --> 00:57:01.740
But the truth is we are not.

00:57:01.740 --> 00:57:04.000
This is actually not something
that people use today.

00:57:04.000 --> 00:57:06.500
People use something
differently.

00:57:06.500 --> 00:57:13.180
And the reason why that is the
case is if you think about it,

00:57:13.180 --> 00:57:19.460
you care about where
tokens are positioned

00:57:19.460 --> 00:57:21.160
in a very specific layer.

00:57:21.160 --> 00:57:25.180
You only care about it
in the attention layer,

00:57:25.180 --> 00:57:28.160
because the attention layer is
the layer where you actually

00:57:28.160 --> 00:57:29.460
are communicating.

00:57:29.460 --> 00:57:33.560
You're trying to determine
which tokens are relevant when

00:57:33.560 --> 00:57:37.840
you do your expression.

00:57:37.840 --> 00:57:43.200
And so this is the reason
why people have actually

00:57:43.200 --> 00:57:49.440
tried to find a method that is
not affecting all the layers,

00:57:49.440 --> 00:57:55.840
but that is being surgically
applied to the attention layer.

00:57:55.840 --> 00:57:58.720
Yeah.

00:57:58.720 --> 00:58:03.280
So the question is if you
do that, then you may make

00:58:03.280 --> 00:58:05.640
further token interact less.

00:58:05.640 --> 00:58:07.340
And that was maybe
the problem of RNN.

00:58:07.340 --> 00:58:08.720
So why are we doing this?

00:58:08.720 --> 00:58:13.760
So here the idea is to inject
some position information

00:58:13.760 --> 00:58:18.540
and intuitively tokens
that are farther apart,

00:58:18.540 --> 00:58:21.440
they're just less relevant.

00:58:21.440 --> 00:58:24.240
But they're not relevant.

00:58:24.240 --> 00:58:30.460
And so here the idea is
that on a relative sense,

00:58:30.460 --> 00:58:34.380
closer token are just more
likely to be more relevant.

00:58:34.380 --> 00:58:37.120
But you're still keeping
the direct connections,

00:58:37.120 --> 00:58:39.740
and you're still giving
a chance to your model

00:58:39.740 --> 00:58:42.220
to actually interact
with those tokens.

00:58:42.220 --> 00:58:45.060
So I do get your concern.

00:58:45.060 --> 00:58:47.580
That being said,
this is something

00:58:47.580 --> 00:58:53.700
that proved to work well,
and it just reflects the idea

00:58:53.700 --> 00:58:57.140
that if let's say you have
a 10 document page, then

00:58:57.140 --> 00:58:59.300
probably what you have
in the same sentence

00:58:59.300 --> 00:59:01.340
is probably more relevant
than something that

00:59:01.340 --> 00:59:04.940
is very far apart, but
you're still giving a chance

00:59:04.940 --> 00:59:06.440
to these tokens to interact.

00:59:09.580 --> 00:59:12.140
OK, great.

00:59:12.140 --> 00:59:13.560
So going back to here.

00:59:13.560 --> 00:59:17.140
So one major drawback
of the previous approach

00:59:17.140 --> 00:59:20.420
is that this position
embedding was actually

00:59:20.420 --> 00:59:25.080
being added to the token
embedding, which was then being

00:59:25.080 --> 00:59:27.160
processed by all the layers.

00:59:27.160 --> 00:59:30.580
But intuitively, this is
actually not what you want,

00:59:30.580 --> 00:59:34.680
because what you want is
to let your attention layer

00:59:34.680 --> 00:59:39.080
know when two tokens
are far apart.

00:59:39.080 --> 00:59:43.760
But you don't want to let your
feedforward neural network know

00:59:43.760 --> 00:59:47.600
that your token is at
position 5 or at position 12

00:59:47.600 --> 00:59:51.360
because the feedforward
neural network doesn't care.

00:59:51.360 --> 00:59:55.160
Can very well have a
sentence where a token is

00:59:55.160 --> 00:59:59.160
at position 24 versus 1.

00:59:59.160 --> 01:00:01.920
That can be very making
sense just because you

01:00:01.920 --> 01:00:05.240
have multiple sentences.

01:00:05.240 --> 01:00:08.200
So this is the reason
why people have

01:00:08.200 --> 01:00:14.320
tried to mitigate this
drawback by surgically looking

01:00:14.320 --> 01:00:19.360
into the attention layers
and doing something there.

01:00:19.360 --> 01:00:22.980
And in particular, I'll
go through a few methods

01:00:22.980 --> 01:00:24.900
that people have tried.

01:00:24.900 --> 01:00:30.860
So one of them is a method
which was meant to learn a bias

01:00:30.860 --> 01:00:34.040
term within the Softmax.

01:00:34.040 --> 01:00:36.060
So if you remember in
the attention layer,

01:00:36.060 --> 01:00:39.700
you have this formula which
I recommend you remember,

01:00:39.700 --> 01:00:45.100
which is Softmax of qk transpose
over some scaling constant times

01:00:45.100 --> 01:00:46.660
v.

01:00:46.660 --> 01:00:49.220
So within the Softmax,
you're actually

01:00:49.220 --> 01:00:54.460
trying to ask yourself
how relevant the query is

01:00:54.460 --> 01:00:56.500
to all the other keys.

01:00:56.500 --> 01:00:58.980
So this is where that matters.

01:00:58.980 --> 01:01:03.820
And so here the idea
is to learn a bias that

01:01:03.820 --> 01:01:11.700
is dependent on how far your
token m and your token n

01:01:11.700 --> 01:01:16.860
are by learning a bias.

01:01:16.860 --> 01:01:19.780
The problem with this is,
so similar to the very first

01:01:19.780 --> 01:01:22.310
method, you need
to learn the bias.

01:01:22.310 --> 01:01:25.110
And so first of all,
maybe your training data

01:01:25.110 --> 01:01:27.490
has some bias in itself.

01:01:27.490 --> 01:01:29.730
So it will maybe not optimal.

01:01:29.730 --> 01:01:31.750
But then the second
thing is it's not

01:01:31.750 --> 01:01:37.590
generalizable to all
possible distances.

01:01:37.590 --> 01:01:40.090
So that's why people have
tried other methods as well.

01:01:40.090 --> 01:01:44.630
Another one is called ALiBi--
so Attention with Linear Biases.

01:01:44.630 --> 01:01:47.110
And so here the idea
is to actually make

01:01:47.110 --> 01:01:52.030
that bias vary as
a function of m--

01:01:52.030 --> 01:01:53.530
sorry, n minus m.

01:01:53.530 --> 01:01:58.310
So the distance between m and n.

01:01:58.310 --> 01:02:04.130
But the method that is commonly
used nowadays is not ALiBi,

01:02:04.130 --> 01:02:06.790
is not the T5 bias, it's RoPE.

01:02:06.790 --> 01:02:10.110
So who has heard of RoPE?

01:02:10.110 --> 01:02:10.990
Yeah?

01:02:10.990 --> 01:02:11.490
OK, great.

01:02:11.490 --> 01:02:15.190
So we're going to see why
RoPE is the way it is.

01:02:15.190 --> 01:02:21.130
So RoPE stands for Rotary
Position Embedding.

01:02:21.130 --> 01:02:27.490
And the idea behind RoPE
is in the attention layer,

01:02:27.490 --> 01:02:29.955
you're not going to add a bias.

01:02:29.955 --> 01:02:31.330
What you're going
to do is you're

01:02:31.330 --> 01:02:35.770
going to change the query
and the key directly.

01:02:35.770 --> 01:02:38.610
And so how are we going
to change them directly?

01:02:38.610 --> 01:02:40.690
What you're going
to do is you're

01:02:40.690 --> 01:02:44.110
going to rotate your query.

01:02:44.110 --> 01:02:49.570
You're going to rotate
your key, each of them

01:02:49.570 --> 01:02:54.130
as a function of the
position that they're in.

01:02:54.130 --> 01:02:57.730
And why is it something
that is very appealing?

01:02:57.730 --> 01:03:02.450
Well, because the nice thing is
when you rotate the query, when

01:03:02.450 --> 01:03:07.410
you rotate the key, what you're
actually doing, when you do

01:03:07.410 --> 01:03:16.290
the self-attention formula is
you're actually bubbling up

01:03:16.290 --> 01:03:21.870
an expression that is a function
of the difference between n

01:03:21.870 --> 01:03:22.450
and m.

01:03:25.070 --> 01:03:27.510
So we're going to see a
little bit how that works.

01:03:27.510 --> 01:03:29.910
So here, so I'm not
sure if any of you

01:03:29.910 --> 01:03:35.010
is an expert at geometry
and rotation matrices.

01:03:35.010 --> 01:03:41.230
But the way you rotate a
vector is you take that vector

01:03:41.230 --> 01:03:45.310
and you actually have
a rotation matrix

01:03:45.310 --> 01:03:48.110
that you apply to that vector.

01:03:48.110 --> 01:03:51.270
So I'm going to maybe
just write that down.

01:03:51.270 --> 01:03:53.570
If I do have this.

01:03:53.570 --> 01:04:00.590
So let's suppose you have
some vector, let's call it x.

01:04:00.590 --> 01:04:08.130
So you have, let's say
you have two dimensions--

01:04:08.130 --> 01:04:10.830
let's assume you have x and y.

01:04:10.830 --> 01:04:17.770
And let's assume that
your vector is like this.

01:04:17.770 --> 01:04:22.210
And this is let's
say a radius of r.

01:04:22.210 --> 01:04:26.330
So here the x-axis is x--

01:04:26.330 --> 01:04:32.470
sorry, it's R and here
you have your theta angle,

01:04:32.470 --> 01:04:34.770
cosine of theta.

01:04:34.770 --> 01:04:42.450
And then here you have your y
value, which is R sine theta.

01:04:42.450 --> 01:04:44.910
So let's assume you have this.

01:04:48.330 --> 01:04:51.370
So what I'm only
doing is expressing

01:04:51.370 --> 01:04:56.090
x as a function of some angle
theta that it already has.

01:04:56.090 --> 01:04:58.890
And what I want to
show you is if you

01:04:58.890 --> 01:05:05.010
were to multiply the rotation
matrix with this vector x.

01:05:05.010 --> 01:05:09.330
So let's assume you have a
rotation matrix of let's say

01:05:09.330 --> 01:05:14.330
angle phi, let's assume, which
is-- so I'm going to just copy

01:05:14.330 --> 01:05:15.690
what is on the slides.

01:05:15.690 --> 01:05:27.270
So it's cosine of phi is minus
sine of phi, sine of phi,

01:05:27.270 --> 01:05:30.950
and then cosine of phi.

01:05:30.950 --> 01:05:37.470
So let's assume you actually
multiply your rotation matrix

01:05:37.470 --> 01:05:40.910
with the vector x.

01:05:40.910 --> 01:05:45.910
So for those of you who
love matrix multiplications

01:05:45.910 --> 01:05:51.750
and trigonometry formulas, this
is the best case because I'm

01:05:51.750 --> 01:05:55.270
going to exactly see.

01:05:55.270 --> 01:05:57.890
So here you would do the
matrix multiplication.

01:05:57.890 --> 01:06:01.630
So R times cosine of
theta cosine of phi

01:06:01.630 --> 01:06:05.110
minus R cosine of theta--

01:06:05.110 --> 01:06:10.150
sorry, minus R sine
of phi sine of theta.

01:06:10.150 --> 01:06:10.770
What is this?

01:06:10.770 --> 01:06:15.850
It's R cosine of theta plus phi.

01:06:15.850 --> 01:06:19.390
And you guessed it, the other
one is the same, but with sine.

01:06:30.770 --> 01:06:33.030
So why am I writing all of this?

01:06:33.030 --> 01:06:36.410
What I want to show
you is if you multiply

01:06:36.410 --> 01:06:39.170
your vector with
the rotation matrix,

01:06:39.170 --> 01:06:41.850
you're rotating your vector.

01:06:41.850 --> 01:06:44.410
And this is the reason why
we have this formula written

01:06:44.410 --> 01:06:46.130
on the slide.

01:06:46.130 --> 01:06:52.770
And the nice thing is, if
you rotate your query vector

01:06:52.770 --> 01:06:56.510
by some angle as
a function of m,

01:06:56.510 --> 01:07:00.650
and if you rotate
your key vector

01:07:00.650 --> 01:07:03.770
as a function of
its position n, then

01:07:03.770 --> 01:07:08.290
you obtain a rotation
matrix that is actually

01:07:08.290 --> 01:07:11.530
a function of n minus m.

01:07:11.530 --> 01:07:13.670
So I will spare you the details.

01:07:13.670 --> 01:07:16.450
But if you just do
the multiplication,

01:07:16.450 --> 01:07:18.950
you will see that it works.

01:07:18.950 --> 01:07:22.910
And so the reason
why it is pretty nice

01:07:22.910 --> 01:07:27.330
is because this is
extensible to any positions.

01:07:27.330 --> 01:07:30.030
You're not learning anything.

01:07:30.030 --> 01:07:33.550
And by the way, one other thing
I will say is I just showed you

01:07:33.550 --> 01:07:34.610
the 2D case.

01:07:34.610 --> 01:07:41.730
But in order to generalize that
let's say d-dimensional case,

01:07:41.730 --> 01:07:48.550
you would actually
do that by 2D blocks.

01:07:48.550 --> 01:07:54.510
And you would do the
rotation with angles that

01:07:54.510 --> 01:07:56.250
are a function of the index.

01:07:56.250 --> 01:07:58.590
So to your earlier
question, so here you

01:07:58.590 --> 01:08:03.370
would have also angles
that would be a function,

01:08:03.370 --> 01:08:04.950
let's say if it's
a lower index, it's

01:08:04.950 --> 01:08:08.650
a different angle compared
to if it's a later index.

01:08:11.570 --> 01:08:12.530
Cool.

01:08:12.530 --> 01:08:14.850
And the nice thing
with this method

01:08:14.850 --> 01:08:20.729
is if you take a look at the
order of magnitude of the dot

01:08:20.729 --> 01:08:23.569
product between the
query and the key

01:08:23.569 --> 01:08:27.930
as a function of how far
their positions are, then

01:08:27.930 --> 01:08:33.050
you will see that roughly
it tends to decay as m and n

01:08:33.050 --> 01:08:35.490
are farther apart.

01:08:35.490 --> 01:08:40.609
So it's not strictly
monotonic because, well, it's

01:08:40.609 --> 01:08:41.870
more complicated than that.

01:08:41.870 --> 01:08:45.930
But at a very high
level, it just decays

01:08:45.930 --> 01:08:50.569
and it just reflects
what we want in tokens

01:08:50.569 --> 01:08:53.930
that are very far apart.

01:08:53.930 --> 01:08:56.210
So that was RoPE.

01:08:56.210 --> 01:09:00.290
So in other words, the
original transformer paper

01:09:00.290 --> 01:09:04.729
used position embeddings
that were additive,

01:09:04.729 --> 01:09:09.790
which is in contrast with
nowadays where people use RoPE

01:09:09.790 --> 01:09:13.990
as a way to encode position.

01:09:13.990 --> 01:09:16.189
So this is how people use it.

01:09:16.189 --> 01:09:21.830
And they don't use
the additive position

01:09:21.830 --> 01:09:24.029
that we discussed before.

01:09:24.029 --> 01:09:27.933
So now we're going to talk
about another component that

01:09:27.933 --> 01:09:29.350
is a little bit
different compared

01:09:29.350 --> 01:09:34.350
to the original transformer, and
this one is the attention layer.

01:09:34.350 --> 01:09:38.550
So I mentioned that
in the encoder part,

01:09:38.550 --> 01:09:40.870
everyone can attend to everyone.

01:09:40.870 --> 01:09:44.310
In the decoder
part, a given token

01:09:44.310 --> 01:09:49.189
can attend to itself
and to tokens before it.

01:09:49.189 --> 01:09:52.670
So one natural question
that people have asked

01:09:52.670 --> 01:09:56.670
is, well, do you
really need to attend

01:09:56.670 --> 01:10:01.150
to everyone at every layer?

01:10:01.150 --> 01:10:04.030
Because it's a lot
of computations.

01:10:04.030 --> 01:10:09.730
And the answer is, well, maybe
you don't need to do that.

01:10:09.730 --> 01:10:14.410
And in particular, there
is a specific variation

01:10:14.410 --> 01:10:18.690
that is called the sliding
window attention that

01:10:18.690 --> 01:10:23.490
is actually meant for
you to attend to only up

01:10:23.490 --> 01:10:27.730
to a certain position that
is farther away from you.

01:10:27.730 --> 01:10:31.970
Not everyone, but only
up to a certain level.

01:10:31.970 --> 01:10:34.410
And so here it's
something that people also

01:10:34.410 --> 01:10:38.410
call local attention.

01:10:38.410 --> 01:10:43.170
And what I want to tell you
is you may-- so probably, you

01:10:43.170 --> 01:10:44.990
will probably come
back to me with saying,

01:10:44.990 --> 01:10:47.302
OK, the RNN actually
had this issue.

01:10:47.302 --> 01:10:48.510
Well, why are you doing this?

01:10:48.510 --> 01:10:53.010
So I want to point
out something that I

01:10:53.010 --> 01:10:59.050
hope will be making sense.

01:10:59.050 --> 01:11:03.350
So your model is
not just one block.

01:11:03.350 --> 01:11:05.150
It's not just one
block of the decoder.

01:11:05.150 --> 01:11:07.620
It's actually several blocks.

01:11:07.620 --> 01:11:12.020
So the attention operation
is not just happening once.

01:11:12.020 --> 01:11:15.020
It is happening several times.

01:11:15.020 --> 01:11:20.400
So if you have, let's
say, a sequence of tokens.

01:11:25.580 --> 01:11:32.540
So I'm going to just replicate
exactly these tokens over here

01:11:32.540 --> 01:11:33.960
and let's say over here.

01:11:37.580 --> 01:11:47.180
So let's assume that
this token can only

01:11:47.180 --> 01:11:54.140
interact with itself and
with one token from before.

01:11:54.140 --> 01:11:58.580
Here you only see yourself
and the token before.

01:11:58.580 --> 01:12:02.980
But if you consider that
in a similar fashion

01:12:02.980 --> 01:12:09.200
this token can attend to
itself and the token before,

01:12:09.200 --> 01:12:15.200
well, effectively, this token
is able to see representations

01:12:15.200 --> 01:12:19.340
from tokens that are
beyond the limit.

01:12:22.560 --> 01:12:23.980
Does what I'm
saying making sense?

01:12:23.980 --> 01:12:24.440
Yeah.

01:12:24.440 --> 01:12:24.940
OK.

01:12:24.940 --> 01:12:31.800
So in other words, even though
you are limiting yourself

01:12:31.800 --> 01:12:35.160
in the tokens that you're
able to interact with,

01:12:35.160 --> 01:12:39.600
if you have several
stacked blocks,

01:12:39.600 --> 01:12:41.440
then what you're
actually able to do

01:12:41.440 --> 01:12:46.040
is to interact with
further tokens.

01:12:46.040 --> 01:12:49.900
And for those of us who are also
familiar with the vision world,

01:12:49.900 --> 01:12:53.200
so in case you're familiar with
convolutional neural networks,

01:12:53.200 --> 01:12:58.560
it's a little bit the same
idea as receptive fields.

01:12:58.560 --> 01:13:02.660
So here when you
perform the convolution,

01:13:02.660 --> 01:13:08.180
a given element is actually a
function of actually many pixels

01:13:08.180 --> 01:13:10.500
if you go back into the chain.

01:13:10.500 --> 01:13:13.220
So it's the same idea.

01:13:13.220 --> 01:13:18.700
OK, I want to tell you something
else about the attention layer.

01:13:18.700 --> 01:13:23.260
So I think we briefly discussed
it earlier in the lecture.

01:13:23.260 --> 01:13:25.760
But OK, I'm going to reuse this.

01:13:29.660 --> 01:13:36.260
So let's assume that we're
back with our decoder

01:13:36.260 --> 01:13:41.580
only model which is typically
what LLMs are composed of.

01:13:41.580 --> 01:13:47.420
And let's assume that
what you want to do

01:13:47.420 --> 01:13:50.620
is to predict the next token.

01:13:50.620 --> 01:13:53.140
Well, this is your
last predicted token.

01:13:53.140 --> 01:13:55.260
In order to predict
the next token,

01:13:55.260 --> 01:13:57.980
you have a bunch of layers, but
in particular in the attention

01:13:57.980 --> 01:14:01.740
layer, what you're doing is
you're attending to yourself.

01:14:01.740 --> 01:14:04.640
You're attending to the
token before, the token

01:14:04.640 --> 01:14:07.880
before that, the
token before that.

01:14:07.880 --> 01:14:11.120
Let's assume you
predict a new token

01:14:11.120 --> 01:14:13.700
and that the new token, in
order to predict the next token,

01:14:13.700 --> 01:14:15.200
you're attending
to yourself, you're

01:14:15.200 --> 01:14:18.340
attending to the one before
you, and so on and so forth.

01:14:21.280 --> 01:14:27.760
So every time you want to
predict the next token, what

01:14:27.760 --> 01:14:32.000
you're doing is this
causal attention,

01:14:32.000 --> 01:14:37.880
where you're comparing
the query from your token

01:14:37.880 --> 01:14:43.520
with all the keys
from the other tokens.

01:14:43.520 --> 01:14:45.980
And every time you want
to predict a new token,

01:14:45.980 --> 01:14:52.400
you again compare it to the
keys of the previous tokens.

01:14:52.400 --> 01:14:59.300
So you could either compute all
the previous keys every time

01:14:59.300 --> 01:15:06.700
you do that operation, or
you could store the keys

01:15:06.700 --> 01:15:09.220
as you perform your decoding.

01:15:09.220 --> 01:15:11.240
So, for instance, when
I decode when I'm here,

01:15:11.240 --> 01:15:12.520
I attend only to myself.

01:15:12.520 --> 01:15:13.740
I computed the key.

01:15:13.740 --> 01:15:16.260
You can save the key.

01:15:16.260 --> 01:15:17.520
You predict this one.

01:15:17.520 --> 01:15:20.035
So here I attend to
here and to the one

01:15:20.035 --> 01:15:21.160
before-- so I need the key.

01:15:21.160 --> 01:15:23.100
I can reuse the key.

01:15:23.100 --> 01:15:26.500
And I compute the
key here that I save.

01:15:26.500 --> 01:15:29.000
And I'm continuing this
process so on and so forth.

01:15:29.000 --> 01:15:37.180
So the problem with this is
keys detect memory space.

01:15:37.180 --> 01:15:40.500
And as your decoding
gets longer and longer,

01:15:40.500 --> 01:15:43.380
you need to store these keys.

01:15:43.380 --> 01:15:45.820
And so if you remember,
the attention layer

01:15:45.820 --> 01:15:48.700
is actually a multi-head
attention layer.

01:15:48.700 --> 01:15:50.300
So it doesn't only
have one key, it

01:15:50.300 --> 01:15:53.340
has actually several
keys for the same layer

01:15:53.340 --> 01:15:55.420
for the same token.

01:15:55.420 --> 01:16:00.880
So that is the reason why
people have tried saving up

01:16:00.880 --> 01:16:03.040
the number of keys and
the number of values

01:16:03.040 --> 01:16:05.800
that each token
needs to produce.

01:16:05.800 --> 01:16:07.940
And that's why you
have this method.

01:16:07.940 --> 01:16:13.000
So, for instance, the idea
of sharing keys and values

01:16:13.000 --> 01:16:15.120
across heads.

01:16:15.120 --> 01:16:18.520
So the reason why people are
interested in such methods

01:16:18.520 --> 01:16:23.040
is because given that you
always need the keys in order

01:16:23.040 --> 01:16:30.280
to predict the next token, you
want to save memory in there.

01:16:30.280 --> 01:16:32.880
And so that is the reason why
you have a number of methods

01:16:32.880 --> 01:16:35.920
like group query
attention, which

01:16:35.920 --> 01:16:39.080
shares the key and
the value projection

01:16:39.080 --> 01:16:44.253
matrices across the heads,
or the group query attention

01:16:44.253 --> 01:16:45.920
which is a little bit
more common, where

01:16:45.920 --> 01:16:51.720
you have a certain number of
groups where you share the key

01:16:51.720 --> 01:16:54.400
and value matrices.

01:16:54.400 --> 01:16:58.060
And this contrasts with the
original multi-head attention

01:16:58.060 --> 01:17:03.820
layer, which had one heads,
where you had all the projection

01:17:03.820 --> 01:17:07.620
matrices that you had to learn.

01:17:07.620 --> 01:17:11.660
So all of that to say
that given that you

01:17:11.660 --> 01:17:17.780
are in this situation where
you're always saving your keys

01:17:17.780 --> 01:17:21.420
and your values for tokens
that you have decoded

01:17:21.420 --> 01:17:24.260
in the past, what
you want is to not

01:17:24.260 --> 01:17:26.720
have to save too much of them.

01:17:26.720 --> 01:17:29.340
You don't want to
saturate your memory.

01:17:29.340 --> 01:17:33.660
So people are trying to
find ways to optimize that.

01:17:33.660 --> 01:17:37.100
And one such way
is to just reduce

01:17:37.100 --> 01:17:39.620
the number of different
projection matrices

01:17:39.620 --> 01:17:43.340
that you need to learn for
the keys and the values.

01:17:43.340 --> 01:17:45.442
And that is the
reason why, I mean,

01:17:45.442 --> 01:17:47.400
no one cares about the
query because the query,

01:17:47.400 --> 01:17:50.200
you use it once and then
you don't need that.

01:17:52.980 --> 01:17:54.200
So does that make sense?

01:17:56.880 --> 01:17:59.860
So nowadays people
use GQA quite a lot.

01:17:59.860 --> 01:18:03.140
So among everything we've seen,
you will see a RoPE quite a lot.

01:18:03.140 --> 01:18:06.700
You will see local attention
with sliding window quite a lot.

01:18:06.700 --> 01:18:10.420
You will see GQA quite a lot.

01:18:10.420 --> 01:18:13.020
And there is one last thing that
I want to talk to you about,

01:18:13.020 --> 01:18:17.400
which is the normalization.

01:18:17.400 --> 01:18:21.445
So in the original
transformer architecture.

01:18:21.445 --> 01:18:23.320
So if you remember, so
it's a little bit more

01:18:23.320 --> 01:18:25.540
of a detail when you
first read the paper.

01:18:25.540 --> 01:18:31.880
But the way people do
normalization is they first

01:18:31.880 --> 01:18:40.040
perform the sublayer operation
and then they add to the inputs

01:18:40.040 --> 01:18:41.460
and then they normalize.

01:18:41.460 --> 01:18:45.960
So they do normalization
of x plus sublayer of x.

01:18:45.960 --> 01:18:48.340
So it's also called post-norm.

01:18:48.340 --> 01:18:52.600
So you're normalizing
after the operation.

01:18:52.600 --> 01:18:55.820
And the reason
why people thought

01:18:55.820 --> 01:18:58.900
it was maybe not the
best way is because here

01:18:58.900 --> 01:19:00.980
the idea behind
residual connection

01:19:00.980 --> 01:19:05.940
is that you want to let
the input through just

01:19:05.940 --> 01:19:07.900
for the gradient to flow better.

01:19:07.900 --> 01:19:11.680
And so here the x is actually
within the layer normalization.

01:19:11.680 --> 01:19:13.860
So it's not great.

01:19:13.860 --> 01:19:17.140
And so this is the reason why
people nowadays, what they do

01:19:17.140 --> 01:19:19.520
is they don't normalize
after the operation.

01:19:19.520 --> 01:19:21.860
They actually normalize before.

01:19:21.860 --> 01:19:24.060
So they first
normalize the inputs

01:19:24.060 --> 01:19:26.200
and then they put
it into the layer.

01:19:26.200 --> 01:19:28.700
And then they add it to x.

01:19:28.700 --> 01:19:31.140
It's also called pre-norm.

01:19:31.140 --> 01:19:33.500
And it's just a way for
the residual connection

01:19:33.500 --> 01:19:39.140
to do its job of letting
the gradient flow.

01:19:39.140 --> 01:19:42.540
So you will see pre-norm as
also a very common method.

01:19:42.540 --> 01:19:47.420
And one last thing, I
promise, which is the layer

01:19:47.420 --> 01:19:49.740
normalization itself.

01:19:49.740 --> 01:19:52.910
So in the original
transformer, here we're

01:19:52.910 --> 01:19:57.990
normalizing across all the
activation by first recentering

01:19:57.990 --> 01:20:02.550
x and then having a
term, gamma, and then

01:20:02.550 --> 01:20:06.150
some bias beta, which helps
with training stability

01:20:06.150 --> 01:20:08.150
and convergence.

01:20:08.150 --> 01:20:11.970
But people have tried with
other normalization methods.

01:20:11.970 --> 01:20:16.190
And in particular, they
have found that RMS norm--

01:20:16.190 --> 01:20:19.270
RMS stands for Root
Mean Squared norm,

01:20:19.270 --> 01:20:22.630
actually does the
job just as well.

01:20:22.630 --> 01:20:25.410
And here, you don't
need to recenter x,

01:20:25.410 --> 01:20:29.150
you just need to rescale
it and to have just one

01:20:29.150 --> 01:20:32.430
factor that you need to learn.

01:20:32.430 --> 01:20:36.230
And that is the reason
why people tend to use it.

01:20:36.230 --> 01:20:38.790
And I think this was
all the tricks I had.

01:20:38.790 --> 01:20:41.990
So just as a summary.

01:20:41.990 --> 01:20:44.390
Before on normalization
front, people

01:20:44.390 --> 01:20:47.990
used to do post-norm
and layer norm,

01:20:47.990 --> 01:20:52.930
and nowadays people do
pre-norm and RMS norm.

01:20:52.930 --> 01:21:02.930
So long story short, nowadays
RoPE, local attention, gka GQA,

01:21:02.930 --> 01:21:07.770
pre-norm, and RMS norm.

01:21:07.770 --> 01:21:11.930
And with that, I'll
give it to Shervin.

01:21:11.930 --> 01:21:13.330
Thank you Afshin.

01:21:13.330 --> 01:21:16.370
So now we're going to
spend some time together

01:21:16.370 --> 01:21:19.850
to talk about next
token prediction.

01:21:19.850 --> 01:21:23.010
And as Afshin has
mentioned, LLMs

01:21:23.010 --> 01:21:25.930
they use this
autoregressive process

01:21:25.930 --> 01:21:28.370
where you start with a
given set of tokens that

01:21:28.370 --> 01:21:31.850
have been processed, and
then you generate the others

01:21:31.850 --> 01:21:33.810
one at a time.

01:21:33.810 --> 01:21:37.170
So let's say you
start with just BOS.

01:21:37.170 --> 01:21:42.090
So you generate A and then
teddy, bear, is, and then

01:21:42.090 --> 01:21:44.330
you complete the sequence.

01:21:44.330 --> 01:21:48.810
So now the question is, how do
you generate the next token?

01:21:48.810 --> 01:21:53.430
So you know that all
of these are processed

01:21:53.430 --> 01:21:55.490
through these decoder layers.

01:21:55.490 --> 01:21:57.950
So you have the
self-attention mechanism

01:21:57.950 --> 01:22:00.230
and the feedforward
neural network

01:22:00.230 --> 01:22:04.750
that is repeated n times because
you have n layers of decoder

01:22:04.750 --> 01:22:06.510
that are stacked.

01:22:06.510 --> 01:22:10.710
But then at the end, it is
projected on a linear layer

01:22:10.710 --> 01:22:16.070
to predict the next token on
the space of your vocabulary.

01:22:16.070 --> 01:22:20.310
And there might be
several ways to choose it.

01:22:20.310 --> 01:22:25.150
So what could be a natural way?

01:22:25.150 --> 01:22:28.730
So first, since you have a
probability distribution,

01:22:28.730 --> 01:22:32.950
one piece of intuition could
be the probability distribution

01:22:32.950 --> 01:22:34.570
has done a lot of work for me.

01:22:34.570 --> 01:22:37.690
It says, what is the
most probable token?

01:22:37.690 --> 01:22:39.110
So let's just take it.

01:22:39.110 --> 01:22:42.230
So that could be one.

01:22:42.230 --> 01:22:48.210
But the thing is, as you compute
tokens one after the other,

01:22:48.210 --> 01:22:53.470
you will try to optimize
the next token immediately.

01:22:53.470 --> 01:22:56.290
But as you have generated
the whole sequence,

01:22:56.290 --> 01:22:59.690
you see that the global
sequence might not be

01:22:59.690 --> 01:23:02.730
with optimal joint probability.

01:23:02.730 --> 01:23:07.610
So this is one weakness
of this approach.

01:23:07.610 --> 01:23:11.130
And you might be tempted
to go the joint probability

01:23:11.130 --> 01:23:12.630
route a bit more.

01:23:12.630 --> 01:23:17.450
So this is why you have
techniques like beam search

01:23:17.450 --> 01:23:20.050
that doesn't do full joint
probability because that

01:23:20.050 --> 01:23:23.370
would be too
computationally expensive.

01:23:23.370 --> 01:23:27.050
But it does track
k path at a time.

01:23:27.050 --> 01:23:29.970
So let's say you want to
compute the next token,

01:23:29.970 --> 01:23:32.870
instead of only
selecting the next one,

01:23:32.870 --> 01:23:36.890
you will consider k
path all the time.

01:23:36.890 --> 01:23:39.270
So here you have
beginning of sequence.

01:23:39.270 --> 01:23:44.390
You have an there that are
good like the two best ones.

01:23:44.390 --> 01:23:47.530
And then you generate the
next probability distribution,

01:23:47.530 --> 01:23:55.430
and then you keep on keeping
the k best and so on.

01:23:55.430 --> 01:23:58.750
So who can tell me,
what kind of weakness

01:23:58.750 --> 01:24:01.150
you see out of
these two techniques

01:24:01.150 --> 01:24:02.970
that I haven't
called out just yet?

01:24:07.610 --> 01:24:08.110
Yes.

01:24:08.110 --> 01:24:11.170
So there is a more computation.

01:24:11.170 --> 01:24:13.370
Yes, for the second one,
but not for the first one.

01:24:13.370 --> 01:24:18.110
I'm looking for something that
is common to both techniques.

01:24:18.110 --> 01:24:25.310
So what can you say
about the reproducibility

01:24:25.310 --> 01:24:29.430
of choosing always the
highest probability token?

01:24:29.430 --> 01:24:32.150
It will always be the same.

01:24:32.150 --> 01:24:35.990
What can you say
about beam search?

01:24:35.990 --> 01:24:38.870
Let's say you redo the
computations at the same time.

01:24:38.870 --> 01:24:41.890
Do you get the same path
each time or will it change?

01:24:45.090 --> 01:24:46.350
Yeah, great.

01:24:46.350 --> 01:24:46.850
So yeah.

01:24:46.850 --> 01:24:49.190
Same path and it's
deterministic.

01:24:49.190 --> 01:24:49.690
Great.

01:24:52.850 --> 01:24:56.650
And this is where I want to
bring your intuition on why that

01:24:56.650 --> 01:24:59.170
may not be great,
because as someone

01:24:59.170 --> 01:25:03.250
who optimizes things for
a living, you might say,

01:25:03.250 --> 01:25:05.270
OK, I don't mind.

01:25:05.270 --> 01:25:07.130
Determinism might be good.

01:25:07.130 --> 01:25:09.410
But I just want to
show you as a user

01:25:09.410 --> 01:25:11.530
why you would care about it.

01:25:11.530 --> 01:25:16.450
So let's suppose you are a
chatbot user of all our favorite

01:25:16.450 --> 01:25:20.210
LLMs, and you ask
a given question,

01:25:20.210 --> 01:25:24.830
like let's say a greeting
message, "Good, and you?"

01:25:24.830 --> 01:25:29.190
And let's say your friends
ask the same message

01:25:29.190 --> 01:25:32.090
and you have the same
answer, and so on, you

01:25:32.090 --> 01:25:36.170
might feel that the
chatbot is robotic.

01:25:36.170 --> 01:25:38.830
And robotic chatbots,
I don't know about you,

01:25:38.830 --> 01:25:43.270
but it's not so appealing
because it feels like it's still

01:25:43.270 --> 01:25:46.910
heavily engineered,
whereas you as a human,

01:25:46.910 --> 01:25:49.830
you might be tempted to
respond a bit differently

01:25:49.830 --> 01:25:51.830
to similar prompts.

01:25:51.830 --> 01:25:55.850
So what I said here is
mostly a thought experiment,

01:25:55.850 --> 01:25:59.110
because in practice,
the context of the LLM

01:25:59.110 --> 01:26:00.650
might change quite a bit.

01:26:00.650 --> 01:26:03.950
Let's say today we are the
2nd of October at Stanford.

01:26:03.950 --> 01:26:06.870
If I ask this question
one hour from now,

01:26:06.870 --> 01:26:08.930
the context will change a bit.

01:26:08.930 --> 01:26:12.370
It might put my new time and
other pieces of information.

01:26:12.370 --> 01:26:15.270
So this might not
strictly apply.

01:26:15.270 --> 01:26:18.870
But still you see
this robotic behavior.

01:26:18.870 --> 01:26:22.630
Whereas non-deterministic
behavior

01:26:22.630 --> 01:26:26.310
could give similarly
well-sounding

01:26:26.310 --> 01:26:30.910
answers as potential
choices, which is great.

01:26:30.910 --> 01:26:36.110
So I hope I convinced you that
determinism is not the best.

01:26:36.110 --> 01:26:38.170
So it can be good
for some use cases.

01:26:38.170 --> 01:26:42.450
I'll talk about it in a bit.

01:26:42.450 --> 01:26:47.570
And one natural next step
to combat determinism

01:26:47.570 --> 01:26:51.130
is to go back to this
probability distribution

01:26:51.130 --> 01:26:54.650
and simply sample from it,
because you have a probability

01:26:54.650 --> 01:26:56.930
distribution, you have weights.

01:26:56.930 --> 01:26:59.890
All our computational
methods enable

01:26:59.890 --> 01:27:01.270
us to sample the next word.

01:27:01.270 --> 01:27:03.130
So let's just do that.

01:27:03.130 --> 01:27:08.290
And you can do so
with two methods,

01:27:08.290 --> 01:27:11.690
because you might not want to
deal with the whole distribution

01:27:11.690 --> 01:27:12.770
all the time.

01:27:12.770 --> 01:27:17.810
So you might want to
select amongst top-k most

01:27:17.810 --> 01:27:20.210
probable next tokens.

01:27:20.210 --> 01:27:24.850
And the second technique
that is used quite often

01:27:24.850 --> 01:27:27.930
is top-p where
you want to select

01:27:27.930 --> 01:27:30.970
tokens that have a
given probability

01:27:30.970 --> 01:27:32.950
distribution in cumulate.

01:27:36.650 --> 01:27:40.550
So I promised you
that I would tell you

01:27:40.550 --> 01:27:42.490
when determinism is needed.

01:27:42.490 --> 01:27:46.590
So, for example, if you perform
natural language tasks that

01:27:46.590 --> 01:27:50.068
are tricky and need
reproducibility,

01:27:50.068 --> 01:27:52.610
for example, let's say you want
to summarize a piece of text.

01:27:52.610 --> 01:27:55.630
You want the same output all
the time, that can be good.

01:27:55.630 --> 01:27:57.470
In one of the later
lectures, we're

01:27:57.470 --> 01:28:01.350
going to talk about evaluations
and particularly LLM

01:28:01.350 --> 01:28:04.990
as a judge, where you want
consistency between the ratings

01:28:04.990 --> 01:28:05.890
that you give.

01:28:05.890 --> 01:28:08.390
So these two are good examples.

01:28:12.150 --> 01:28:16.110
In the rest of the time,
this non-deterministic path

01:28:16.110 --> 01:28:17.530
might be the most attractive.

01:28:20.070 --> 01:28:24.030
So now I'm going to
go into the specifics

01:28:24.030 --> 01:28:25.650
that I mentioned
on a high-level,

01:28:25.650 --> 01:28:27.570
but making sure
that it makes sense.

01:28:27.570 --> 01:28:31.550
So in the last layers, you
have this linear projection

01:28:31.550 --> 01:28:35.870
between d model, the
embedding space of your token

01:28:35.870 --> 01:28:37.330
at a given time.

01:28:37.330 --> 01:28:41.690
And then you project it on
the space of vocabulary.

01:28:41.690 --> 01:28:47.970
And then based on this, you
apply this Softmax operator

01:28:47.970 --> 01:28:52.450
to convert these numbers into
a probability distribution.

01:28:52.450 --> 01:28:54.610
And why am I telling you that?

01:28:54.610 --> 01:28:59.050
It's because I want to introduce
a parameter that you might have

01:28:59.050 --> 01:29:02.250
heard as users quite
bit-- the temperature

01:29:02.250 --> 01:29:07.090
T, which helps modulate
the probability

01:29:07.090 --> 01:29:09.090
distribution that you get.

01:29:09.090 --> 01:29:16.410
So here, as you see, so the
regular Softmax operator

01:29:16.410 --> 01:29:17.830
has T equals 1.

01:29:17.830 --> 01:29:20.530
So the formula that you see
on the right is T equals 1.

01:29:20.530 --> 01:29:23.130
You introduce T like so.

01:29:23.130 --> 01:29:29.290
I want to ask your intuition
of how you feel the probability

01:29:29.290 --> 01:29:31.750
distribution to be then.

01:29:31.750 --> 01:29:35.330
So let's say you
take T bigger than 1.

01:29:35.330 --> 01:29:38.020
If you have a given
probability distribution,

01:29:38.020 --> 01:29:42.120
how do you feel the probability
distribution will change?

01:29:46.540 --> 01:29:48.560
So yeah, the one
comment is flatter.

01:29:48.560 --> 01:29:49.580
Yeah, great.

01:29:49.580 --> 01:29:51.740
And let me ask you the opposite.

01:29:51.740 --> 01:29:53.500
Let's say you have very small t.

01:29:53.500 --> 01:29:55.460
What is your intuition?

01:29:55.460 --> 01:29:57.240
Yeah and the comment is sharper.

01:29:57.240 --> 01:29:58.300
Yeah, great.

01:29:58.300 --> 01:30:01.100
So is it clear to everyone?

01:30:01.100 --> 01:30:06.740
Because if you have small
T, then the Xi that is high

01:30:06.740 --> 01:30:10.400
will be more emphasized.

01:30:10.400 --> 01:30:14.980
And the for the other i's,
the some at the bottom

01:30:14.980 --> 01:30:15.960
will take it over.

01:30:15.960 --> 01:30:18.980
So you will have just one term--

01:30:18.980 --> 01:30:21.140
so you will have
just one index that

01:30:21.140 --> 01:30:23.560
will have a probability
close to one

01:30:23.560 --> 01:30:25.340
and the other ones
will be close to 0.

01:30:25.340 --> 01:30:29.820
And when T is large, then
all of these x exponential

01:30:29.820 --> 01:30:33.520
of something over T will tend
to 0 within the exponential.

01:30:33.520 --> 01:30:37.600
So everything will be
more or less uniform.

01:30:37.600 --> 01:30:40.320
And this is exactly
what these graphs show.

01:30:40.320 --> 01:30:44.520
And one thing, so
this is a determinism

01:30:44.520 --> 01:30:49.380
is continuous more so than
truly like binary choice.

01:30:49.380 --> 01:30:54.300
So you're not committed to
either turn it on or off.

01:30:54.300 --> 01:30:58.680
You can adjust the
temperature as a setting.

01:30:58.680 --> 01:31:02.800
And I want to say-- so I
talked about determinism.

01:31:02.800 --> 01:31:04.460
Does it really happen?

01:31:04.460 --> 01:31:06.480
So you could think that
it does, because when

01:31:06.480 --> 01:31:09.020
you look at your
decoder architecture,

01:31:09.020 --> 01:31:11.200
you have embeddings
that are learned.

01:31:11.200 --> 01:31:14.240
OK, these are deterministically
set at inference time.

01:31:14.240 --> 01:31:17.540
It goes through the masked
self-attention mechanism.

01:31:17.540 --> 01:31:20.540
You have fixed weights,
fixed operations.

01:31:20.540 --> 01:31:22.940
You could think that's fixed,
feedforward neural network.

01:31:22.940 --> 01:31:25.060
You have given weights
that are fixed.

01:31:25.060 --> 01:31:25.780
You multiply.

01:31:25.780 --> 01:31:29.120
This should be fixed and so on.

01:31:29.120 --> 01:31:31.080
So with a temperature
of 0, you would

01:31:31.080 --> 01:31:34.820
think that this is
completely deterministic.

01:31:34.820 --> 01:31:39.620
But it turns out that not
because in practice LLM

01:31:39.620 --> 01:31:44.660
inference engines, they will
batch your request among others.

01:31:44.660 --> 01:31:48.340
And the reason why
nondeterminism comes into play

01:31:48.340 --> 01:31:50.660
is a purely computational one.

01:31:50.660 --> 01:31:53.380
In theory, it should
still be deterministic.

01:31:53.380 --> 01:31:57.060
But there is this concept
of batch invariance

01:31:57.060 --> 01:32:00.500
that is not respected
in some cases.

01:32:00.500 --> 01:32:03.300
And I strongly
recommend this blog

01:32:03.300 --> 01:32:07.640
from [INAUDIBLE] as a
great piece of reading.

01:32:07.640 --> 01:32:10.140
So he goes through
the exact reason.

01:32:10.140 --> 01:32:13.020
And if you want me to
break it down to you,

01:32:13.020 --> 01:32:19.420
it all goes to the floating
points non-associativity

01:32:19.420 --> 01:32:22.220
with respect to the
addition operation,

01:32:22.220 --> 01:32:29.460
where the order of addition
does matter on the result

01:32:29.460 --> 01:32:32.040
that you get.

01:32:32.040 --> 01:32:37.500
So let's say you add a big
number to a small number

01:32:37.500 --> 01:32:42.040
and then you retract that
big number, you might get 0.

01:32:42.040 --> 01:32:44.040
Whereas if you do that
operation separately,

01:32:44.040 --> 01:32:46.120
you will get that
small number back.

01:32:46.120 --> 01:32:49.040
So it all has to do with the
representation of floating point

01:32:49.040 --> 01:32:51.440
numbers that are approximated.

01:32:51.440 --> 01:32:57.820
So this relationship does apply
in the world of floating points.

01:33:01.740 --> 01:33:02.240
Yeah.

01:33:05.880 --> 01:33:08.480
So the comment here
the tensor should

01:33:08.480 --> 01:33:10.860
be computed independently.

01:33:10.860 --> 01:33:14.040
So it turns out that depending
on the implementation,

01:33:14.040 --> 01:33:19.760
the order of operation within
these tensors will change.

01:33:19.760 --> 01:33:23.600
Yes, that is the reason.

01:33:23.600 --> 01:33:28.360
So yeah, the blog has more
information on exactly why

01:33:28.360 --> 01:33:30.380
and then how to
build kernels that

01:33:30.380 --> 01:33:34.220
do apply this as great reading.

01:33:34.220 --> 01:33:35.320
Any other questions?

01:33:39.300 --> 01:33:41.340
OK, great.

01:33:41.340 --> 01:33:46.900
And so now we talked about
decoding in the usual case.

01:33:46.900 --> 01:33:50.540
Sometimes, and more particularly
in an industry setting,

01:33:50.540 --> 01:33:54.340
you might want to use these
LLMs to perform tasks you would

01:33:54.340 --> 01:33:57.980
have used complex systems for.

01:33:57.980 --> 01:34:02.240
So you might want to generate
let's say structured output,

01:34:02.240 --> 01:34:04.180
such as JSON.

01:34:04.180 --> 01:34:07.180
And one question that
you might ask yourself,

01:34:07.180 --> 01:34:10.460
let's say you want to
generate a piece of data

01:34:10.460 --> 01:34:13.760
in this specific format and
then this matters to you.

01:34:13.760 --> 01:34:15.240
Because if it
doesn't do it right,

01:34:15.240 --> 01:34:18.500
then parsing fails downstream
and you're in trouble.

01:34:18.500 --> 01:34:20.180
How do you do so?

01:34:20.180 --> 01:34:23.660
So one first approach
that you could think about

01:34:23.660 --> 01:34:27.380
is just ask the LLM for
it, do this in JSON format

01:34:27.380 --> 01:34:29.560
and hope for the best.

01:34:29.560 --> 01:34:32.760
With today's models,
even the lightest,

01:34:32.760 --> 01:34:35.000
this usually works well.

01:34:35.000 --> 01:34:38.680
But it's not 100% and
what you want is 100%.

01:34:38.680 --> 01:34:40.920
So how can you do that?

01:34:40.920 --> 01:34:44.240
So there is a concept
called guided decoding,

01:34:44.240 --> 01:34:48.800
where the span of
available tokens

01:34:48.800 --> 01:34:52.680
to be predicted at the
next step is restricted,

01:34:52.680 --> 01:34:56.400
depending on the syntax
of the language you

01:34:56.400 --> 01:34:58.140
want to decode this in.

01:34:58.140 --> 01:34:59.700
So let's say you are in JSON.

01:34:59.700 --> 01:35:03.920
You know That your
first token must

01:35:03.920 --> 01:35:10.080
be a specific symbol and so on.

01:35:10.080 --> 01:35:14.240
And this is how you
could deal with it.

01:35:14.240 --> 01:35:17.320
OK, so I hope all made sense.

01:35:17.320 --> 01:35:20.600
And now we're going to talk
about one exciting part, which

01:35:20.600 --> 01:35:23.640
is prompting.

01:35:23.640 --> 01:35:27.420
And I want to
introduce some terms,

01:35:27.420 --> 01:35:32.540
so that we all know some
basics of vocabulary there.

01:35:32.540 --> 01:35:36.540
So let's say you want to
issue a request to an LLM.

01:35:36.540 --> 01:35:39.620
Usually, you do not
put just the BOS token

01:35:39.620 --> 01:35:42.340
and let it generate
everything afterwards.

01:35:42.340 --> 01:35:44.020
You have a given prompt.

01:35:44.020 --> 01:35:47.580
So here my prompt
could be a teddy bear.

01:35:47.580 --> 01:35:50.340
And I want to generate
the next tokens out of it.

01:35:50.340 --> 01:35:54.580
So what happens is that I
will put all of them as input.

01:35:54.580 --> 01:35:59.500
And then autoregressively, it
will compute each token one

01:35:59.500 --> 01:36:01.340
at a time.

01:36:01.340 --> 01:36:05.900
And you might have heard of this
concept called context window.

01:36:05.900 --> 01:36:09.620
Where does it come at play here?

01:36:09.620 --> 01:36:14.620
So at the stage of next
token prediction, anywhere

01:36:14.620 --> 01:36:19.820
you are, what is as input
is your context window.

01:36:19.820 --> 01:36:23.000
And when you look at
your whole prediction,

01:36:23.000 --> 01:36:26.960
once it's completely done,
whatever tokens were generated

01:36:26.960 --> 01:36:30.080
are called the output tokens.

01:36:30.080 --> 01:36:31.600
So you might be
tempted sometimes

01:36:31.600 --> 01:36:34.720
to say that the context
window is exactly what you

01:36:34.720 --> 01:36:38.700
put as input as a user, but
it actually is more than that,

01:36:38.700 --> 01:36:41.000
because every token
that you generate

01:36:41.000 --> 01:36:44.520
needs to be put back at
the input level, which

01:36:44.520 --> 01:36:47.820
is why when you think
about these large numbers,

01:36:47.820 --> 01:36:50.200
you think I can input anything.

01:36:50.200 --> 01:36:52.040
These are large numbers
as context window,

01:36:52.040 --> 01:36:54.600
as we'll see in the slide.

01:36:54.600 --> 01:36:56.180
You need to take
that into account.

01:36:56.180 --> 01:37:00.280
So it's linked
with output length.

01:37:00.280 --> 01:37:02.840
Yep.

01:37:02.840 --> 01:37:05.240
So the question
is, why if you need

01:37:05.240 --> 01:37:08.640
to have a fixed context length,
like a maximum context length?

01:37:08.640 --> 01:37:10.960
So the reason is a
computational one.

01:37:10.960 --> 01:37:14.520
And we'll see very soon
why it might not even

01:37:14.520 --> 01:37:17.180
be desirable to have
higher context length.

01:37:17.180 --> 01:37:21.132
So there is also an
intuition based one reason.

01:37:21.132 --> 01:37:22.340
We'll it in seen in a second.

01:37:25.140 --> 01:37:27.220
That makes sense?

01:37:27.220 --> 01:37:30.860
So I want to give some
orders of magnitude here.

01:37:30.860 --> 01:37:33.020
So in terms of
context length, you

01:37:33.020 --> 01:37:36.900
might all know about that
O of 1 million tokens

01:37:36.900 --> 01:37:39.880
as input, which is
the industry standard.

01:37:39.880 --> 01:37:43.780
And then as output tokens,
it's usually in the order

01:37:43.780 --> 01:37:46.380
of magnitude of 100k.

01:37:46.380 --> 01:37:49.300
That was true until
two days ago where

01:37:49.300 --> 01:37:51.080
I had to rework this slide.

01:37:51.080 --> 01:37:52.700
Actually, maybe
soon, we're going

01:37:52.700 --> 01:37:55.420
to move into the million
territory there as well.

01:37:55.420 --> 01:38:00.980
And this new LLM that came out
didn't publish an input maximum

01:38:00.980 --> 01:38:01.480
length.

01:38:01.480 --> 01:38:05.420
So maybe we might have gone one
order of magnitude higher there.

01:38:05.420 --> 01:38:08.460
But these are some good
orders of magnitude.

01:38:08.460 --> 01:38:13.060
And then to give you some
intuition on what token means.

01:38:13.060 --> 01:38:21.680
So usually, a token is 3/4 of
a word and a page is about 500

01:38:21.680 --> 01:38:22.560
tokens.

01:38:22.560 --> 01:38:24.900
So when you talk about
orders of magnitude,

01:38:24.900 --> 01:38:29.100
if you take 100k tokens, it's
about a book, and a million,

01:38:29.100 --> 01:38:33.040
it's a large book.

01:38:33.040 --> 01:38:35.480
And now coming back
to your question,

01:38:35.480 --> 01:38:39.080
is it the case that always
having more tokens as input

01:38:39.080 --> 01:38:40.200
is better?

01:38:40.200 --> 01:38:43.920
Well, you need to beware of
a phenomenon that's called

01:38:43.920 --> 01:38:48.240
"context rot," where there
is this test called needle

01:38:48.240 --> 01:38:51.440
in a haystack, where you put a
piece of information that you

01:38:51.440 --> 01:38:55.320
care about somewhere in that
context window and the task is

01:38:55.320 --> 01:38:56.800
a retrieval one.

01:38:56.800 --> 01:39:01.720
And it turns out that as the
context length goes higher,

01:39:01.720 --> 01:39:04.280
the LLM has more
and more difficulty

01:39:04.280 --> 01:39:07.640
to retrieve that
piece of information.

01:39:07.640 --> 01:39:11.220
So sometimes it's a problem
that comes from the LLM itself.

01:39:11.220 --> 01:39:14.240
So you will always see
in the model releases

01:39:14.240 --> 01:39:18.000
the hallucination rate
that goes down and so on.

01:39:18.000 --> 01:39:21.250
But this is not something
that you could perfectly

01:39:21.250 --> 01:39:24.010
solve, because
conceptually, when

01:39:24.010 --> 01:39:27.010
you mix a piece of
information, among others,

01:39:27.010 --> 01:39:29.570
it might be the case that
there is so much noise

01:39:29.570 --> 01:39:31.970
around that the
task of retrieving

01:39:31.970 --> 01:39:36.050
exactly that piece of
information is not realistic.

01:39:36.050 --> 01:39:41.290
And in general, putting
more and more as inputs

01:39:41.290 --> 01:39:45.530
pushes that risk up, which
is why in general you

01:39:45.530 --> 01:39:47.070
hear about context compaction.

01:39:47.070 --> 01:39:49.450
You should start a
new chat every time.

01:39:49.450 --> 01:39:52.290
These things still
apply conceptually.

01:39:52.290 --> 01:39:57.130
And this is why we are not here
with unlimited context window.

01:39:57.130 --> 01:40:00.770
It's also like a good practice.

01:40:00.770 --> 01:40:02.130
OK, awesome.

01:40:02.130 --> 01:40:05.770
And I'm going to spend the
last minutes on techniques

01:40:05.770 --> 01:40:08.890
to improve performance
without you changing

01:40:08.890 --> 01:40:11.130
any weights of your model.

01:40:11.130 --> 01:40:16.730
So there is a large concept
called in-context learning,

01:40:16.730 --> 01:40:21.790
where instead of teaching
in terms of weights tuning

01:40:21.790 --> 01:40:27.970
your LLM, you give examples
and solutions of what you want.

01:40:27.970 --> 01:40:33.470
So let's say you are in the task
of asking your LLM about the age

01:40:33.470 --> 01:40:35.150
of your teddy bear.

01:40:35.150 --> 01:40:40.470
So if you just ask it in zero
shot versus give some question

01:40:40.470 --> 01:40:43.150
answers of what
answer you expect,

01:40:43.150 --> 01:40:49.190
the LLM will learn the format
and maybe even the reasoning

01:40:49.190 --> 01:40:53.110
that it needs to go
through implicitly.

01:40:53.110 --> 01:40:57.390
And these days, you will
see few shot learning

01:40:57.390 --> 01:41:00.330
being less and less used
because the reasoning models,

01:41:00.330 --> 01:41:02.190
they get better and better.

01:41:02.190 --> 01:41:05.830
And the bad effects
of few shot learning

01:41:05.830 --> 01:41:09.750
is that it will make your model
overfit on the specific examples

01:41:09.750 --> 01:41:11.630
that you give it,
whereas what you want

01:41:11.630 --> 01:41:13.830
is be more reasoning-based.

01:41:13.830 --> 01:41:17.130
So you will see some prompt
tuning techniques that

01:41:17.130 --> 01:41:20.230
turn these examples
into instructions,

01:41:20.230 --> 01:41:23.450
and people typically
handcraft such instructions

01:41:23.450 --> 01:41:26.610
and put them
instead of examples.

01:41:26.610 --> 01:41:31.170
And then again, some other
obvious downsides are also

01:41:31.170 --> 01:41:34.090
you have more
input tokens, which

01:41:34.090 --> 01:41:36.250
increases cost and latency.

01:41:36.250 --> 01:41:38.650
So on that reasoning
part, you might

01:41:38.650 --> 01:41:42.610
say, how about we put
some reasoning in there?

01:41:42.610 --> 01:41:48.170
So there is this landmark
paper called chain of thought,

01:41:48.170 --> 01:41:52.930
where it takes in-context
learning to the next stage

01:41:52.930 --> 01:41:57.810
by teaching the model in
these few short prompts how

01:41:57.810 --> 01:41:59.530
it gets there.

01:41:59.530 --> 01:42:04.130
And the authors, they saw that
the model learned the reasoning

01:42:04.130 --> 01:42:08.730
path, and overall it
improved performance.

01:42:08.730 --> 01:42:11.930
And I don't want you to
consider chain of thought

01:42:11.930 --> 01:42:17.090
to be purely an in-context
learning technique.

01:42:17.090 --> 01:42:18.490
It's way more than that.

01:42:18.490 --> 01:42:20.230
And we will talk
about reasoning models

01:42:20.230 --> 01:42:24.210
where thinking tokens are used
and the same concept applies.

01:42:24.210 --> 01:42:26.430
So we'll talk about it then.

01:42:26.430 --> 01:42:31.510
And one technique that one
might be tempted to use offline

01:42:31.510 --> 01:42:33.910
is called self-consistency.

01:42:33.910 --> 01:42:38.710
So how about, instead of
generating one reasoning path, I

01:42:38.710 --> 01:42:40.950
generate several ones?

01:42:40.950 --> 01:42:44.630
Then one nice thing is that it
makes the output that you might

01:42:44.630 --> 01:42:47.750
be interested in, let's say
in the arithmetic world,

01:42:47.750 --> 01:42:49.030
more stable.

01:42:49.030 --> 01:42:53.270
And by majority voting,
you can get to an answer

01:42:53.270 --> 01:42:56.750
where more generations agree to.

01:42:56.750 --> 01:43:00.270
Yeah, we'll see in reasoning
models lectures, more

01:43:00.270 --> 01:43:02.030
about these.

01:43:02.030 --> 01:43:03.750
And that's all I
had for you today.

01:43:03.750 --> 01:43:07.070
And I hope you have
a great weekend.

01:43:07.070 --> 01:43:08.210
Thank you.

01:43:08.210 --> 01:43:12.160
[APPLAUSE]

